= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose a <matrix splitting> $A=M-N$ with $M$ invertible. Then $Ax=b$ is equivalent to
$$
x=M^{-1}Nx+M^{-1}b,
$$
and the associated <stationary iterative method for a linear system> is
$$
x^{(k+1)}=Hx^{(k)}+M^{-1}b,
\qquad H=M^{-1}N.
$$
For the <Jacobi method>, write $A=D+L+U$ with $D$ diagonal and $L,U$ strictly lower and upper triangular, and take
$$
M=D,qquad N=-(L+U).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{x^{(k+1)}=-D^{-1}(L+U)x^{(k)}+D^{-1}b.}
$$
The iteration converges for every initial vector exactly when the <spectral radius> of its iteration matrix satisfies
$$
\boxed{\rho\bigl(-D^{-1}(L+U)\bigr)<1.}
$$

The one-dimensional <isentropic Euler equations> are
$$
\rho_t+u\rho_x+\rho u_x=0,
\qquad
u_t+uu_x+\frac1\rho p_x=0.
$$
For the <polytropic equation of state>,
$$
c^2=\frac{dp}{d\rho}=\frac{\gamma p}{\rho},
\qquad
\frac c{c_0}=\left(\frac\rho{\rho_0}\right)^{(\gamma-1)/2}.
$$
The continuity equation therefore implies
$$
c_t+uc_x+\frac{\gamma-1}{2}cu_x=0,
$$
while the momentum equation becomes
$$
u_t+uu_x+\frac{2c}{\gamma-1}c_x=0.
$$
Adding and subtracting these equations gives
$$
\left[\partial_t+(u\pm c)\partial_x\right]
\left[u\pm\frac{2(c-c_0)}{\gamma-1}\right]=0.
$$
Thus the <Riemann invariants>
$$
\boxed{R_\pm=u\pm\frac{2(c-c_0)}{\gamma-1}}
$$
are constant along the <characteristic curves>
$$
\boxed{C_\pm:\quad\frac{dx}{dt}=u\pm c.}
$$

Let $u(r,z)$ and $w(r,z)$ be the radial and vertical velocity components. For steady incompressible <axisymmetric flow> without swirl, <mass conservation> and the relevant cylindrical components of the <Navier-Stokes equation> are
$$
\frac1r\frac{\partial(ru)}{\partial r}+\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}=0,
$$
$$
\rho\left(u\frac{\partial u}{\partial r}+w\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}\right)
=-\frac{\partial p}{\partial r}
+\mu\left(\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial r^2}+\frac1r\frac{\partial u}{\partial r}-\frac{u}{r^2}+\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial z^2}\right),
$$
$$
\rho\left(u\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}+w\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\right)
=-\frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
+\mu\left(\frac{\partial^2w}{\partial r^2}+\frac1r\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}+\frac{\partial^2w}{\partial z^2}\right).
$$

The vertical scale is $w\sim V$. <Incompressibility> over radial scale $R$ and gap scale $h$ gives $u\sim VR/h=V/\varepsilon$. In the radial equation, inertia is smaller than transverse viscous stress by
$$
\frac{\rho Vh}{\mu}=\operatorname{Re}\ll1,
$$
while radial viscous derivatives are smaller than $u_{zz}$ by $\varepsilon^2$. The pressure scale is $\mu VR^2/h^3$. In the vertical equation, both viscous and inertial terms are smaller than the corresponding cross-gap pressure-gradient scale by at least $O(\varepsilon^2)$. Thus the leading <lubrication theory> equations are
$$
\boxed{\frac1r(ru)_r+w_z=0,
\qquad p_r=\mu u_{zz},
\qquad p_z=0.}
$$

Because the <Levi-Civita connection> is torsion-free, its <Christoffel symbols> satisfy $\Gamma^\mu_{\rho\alpha}=\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\rho}$. Substitute the stated coordinate expression for each term in the cyclic sum
$$
R^\mu{}_{\rho\alpha\beta}
+R^\mu{}_{\beta\rho\alpha}
+R^\mu{}_{\alpha\beta\rho}.
$$
The partial-derivative terms cancel pairwise by the lower-index symmetry of $\Gamma$, and the quadratic terms cancel after relabelling their dummy indices. Therefore
$$
\boxed{R^\mu{}_{\rho\alpha\beta}
+R^\mu{}_{\beta\rho\alpha}
+R^\mu{}_{\alpha\beta\rho}=0,}
$$
which is the <first Bianchi identity>.

Choose the <Landau gauge>
$$
\mathbf A=(0,Bx,0),\qquad \phi=0.
$$
It preserves <translation invariance> in the $y$-direction, so $p_y=\hbar k$ is conserved. With $p_z=0$, <magnetic minimal coupling> gives
$$
H=\frac1{2m}\left[p_x^2+(p_y-qBx)^2\right]
=\frac{p_x^2}{2m}+\frac{m\omega_c^2}{2}(x-x_k)^2,
$$
where
$$
\omega_c=\frac{|qB|}{m},
\qquad
x_k=\frac{\hbar k}{qB}.
$$
This is a <quantum harmonic oscillator>, so the <Landau levels> are
$$
\boxed{E_n=\hbar\omega_c\left(n+\frac12\right),\qquad n=0,1,2,\ldots,}
$$
independent of $k$.

The periodic boundary condition in $y$ quantizes $k=2\pi r/L_y$. Requiring the guiding centre to lie in a strip of width $L_x$ gives a $k$-interval of width $|qB|L_x/\hbar$. The number of allowed integers is therefore approximately
$$
\boxed{D=\frac{L_y}{2\pi}\frac{|qB|L_x}{\hbar}
=\frac{|qB|A}{2\pi\hbar},}
$$
the <degeneracy of a Landau level>, up to boundary corrections.

The <Chinese remainder theorem> says that if the positive integers $m_1,\ldots,m_r$ are pairwise <coprime integers>[coprime] and $M=\prod_i m_i$, then for arbitrary residue classes $a_i\pmod{m_i}$ there is a unique residue class $x\pmod M$ satisfying
$$
x\equiv a_i\pmod{m_i}
\qquad(1\leq i\leq r).
$$
Equivalently, reduction is a <ring isomorphism>
$$
\mathbb Z/M\mathbb Z\longrightarrow
\prod_{i=1}^r\mathbb Z/m_i\mathbb Z.
$$

For existence, put $M_i=M/m_i$. Pairwise coprimality gives $\gcd(M_i,m_i)=1$, so <Bezout identity>[Bézout's identity] supplies an integer $u_i$ with $u_iM_i\equiv1\pmod{m_i}$. Then
$$
x=\sum_{i=1}^r a_i u_iM_i
$$
has the required residues, because every term except the $i$th is divisible by $m_i$. If $x$ and $y$ are two solutions, each $m_i$ divides $x-y$. Pairwise coprimality then implies that their product $M$ divides $x-y$, proving uniqueness modulo $M$.

