= Solution
{parent=i}

The undisturbed gas has $u=0$, $c=c_0$, and hence $R_-=0$. The disturbance from the receding piston is a right-moving <simple wave>, so this invariant remains zero throughout it:
$$
c=c_0+\frac{\gamma-1}{2}u,
\qquad
\rho=\rho_0\left(1+\frac{\gamma-1}{2c_0}u\right)^{2/(\gamma-1)}.
$$

Let $s$ be the time at which the relevant $C_+$ characteristic leaves the piston. The boundary condition is $u=\dot X_p(s)$ there. Both $u$ and $c$ are constant along this characteristic, whose speed is
$$
u+c=c_0+\frac{\gamma+1}{2}\dot X_p(s).
$$
Hence $s=s(x,t)$ is determined implicitly by the <receding-piston rarefaction wave> equation
$$
\boxed{x=X_p(s)+\left[c_0+\frac{\gamma+1}{2}\dot X_p(s)\right](t-s).}
$$
Within $X_p(t)<x<c_0t$, the solution is therefore
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\dot X_p(s(x,t)),}
$$
$$
\boxed{\rho(x,t)=\rho_0
\left[1+\frac{\gamma-1}{2c_0}\dot X_p(s(x,t))\right]^{2/(\gamma-1)}.}
$$
For $x\geq c_0t$, the gas remains undisturbed: $u=0$ and $\rho=\rho_0$. The speed bound keeps $c$ and $\rho$ positive, and monotonic increase of $|\dot X_p|$ makes this an expanding <rarefaction wave> rather than a characteristic-crossing compression.

Write
$$
f_1=xy,
\qquad
f_2=y^2-z^3+xz,
\qquad
f_3=x(x+y+2z+1).
$$
The equation $xy=0$ splits the analysis into two cases. If $x=0$, the remaining equation is $y^2=z^3$, giving the <cuspidal cubic>
$$
C=V(x,y^2-z^3).
$$
It is irreducible because $y^2-z^3$ is irreducible in $k[y,z]$: as a quadratic in $y$, it could factor only if $z^3$ were a square in $k[z]$.

If $y=0$, the other equations become
$$
z(x-z^2)=0,
\qquad
x(x+2z+1)=0.
$$
Their solutions are $(0,0,0)$, which already lies on $C$, and the two points
$$
P=(-1,0,0),
\qquad
Q=(1,0,-1).
$$
Thus the <irreducible components> are
$$
\boxed{C,quad\{P\},\quad\{Q\}}.
$$
Equivalently, the <radical ideal> of the <cusp with two isolated-point components> is
$$
I(V)
=(x,y^2-z^3)
\cap(x+1,y,z)
\cap(x-1,y,z+1).
$$

This language is context-free. It is the language of <context-free grammar for even palindromes>[even-length palindromes] over $\{a,b\}$, generated by the <context-free grammar>
$$
S\to aSa\mid bSb\mid\epsilon.
$$
Every derivation adds matching outer symbols, and removing the matching first and last symbols gives the converse induction.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $t=t_c$, the piston velocity reaches
$$
u_v=-\frac{2c_0}{\gamma-1}.
$$
The simple-wave relation then gives $c=0$ and $\rho=0$. For later times the formal continuation would require negative sound speed, so the gas can no longer follow the piston. By <vacuum formation behind a receding piston>, a vacuum region opens between $X_p(t)$ and the gas edge
$$
\boxed{x_v(t)=X_p(t_c)-\frac{2c_0}{\gamma-1}(t-t_c).}
$$
The earlier rarefaction solution remains to the right of $x_v(t)$, while $X_p(t)<x<x_v(t)$ is vacuum.

Let $(a,b,c)$ be a tangent vector in the $x,y,z$ directions. At a point $(0,y,z)\in C$, the two isolated components are absent in a Zariski neighbourhood, so the <Zariski tangent space> is that of $C$. Differentiating $x$ and $y^2-z^3$ gives
$$
\boxed{
T_{(0,y,z)}V
=\{(a,b,c):a=0,\ 2yb-3z^2c=0\}}.
$$
For $(y,z)\ne(0,0)$ this is a line. At the cusp,
$$
\boxed{T_{(0,0,0)}V=\{(0,b,c):b,c\in k\}},
$$
which is two-dimensional.

Each of $P$ and $Q$ is a reduced isolated component with local ring $k$, so
$$
\boxed{T_PV=T_QV=\{0\}}.
$$
This last conclusion must use all functions in $I(V)$, as required by the definition of the Zariski tangent space. The Jacobian of only the three displayed defining polynomials has an extra spurious tangent direction at $Q$, because those polynomials do not generate the radical ideal there; for example, $x(x^2-1)\in I(V)$ removes that direction.

This language is not context-free. Suppose it had pumping length $N$, and choose a prime $p>N$. Apply the pumping lemma to $w=0^p1^p$. Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be the total numbers of zeros and ones in the pumped pieces $v$ and $y$. If $\alpha\ne\beta$, pumping with $i=0$ produces unequal block lengths and leaves the language. If $\alpha=\beta$, then $|vy|>0$ forces $\alpha=\beta=d>0$. Pumping with $i=p+1$ produces equal block lengths
$$
p+pd=p(1+d),
$$
which are composite. Every possible pumping decomposition fails, proving the <equal prime block language is not context-free>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here
$$
X_p(s)=-\frac{s^{1+\alpha}}{1+\alpha},
\qquad
\dot X_p(s)=-s^\alpha.
$$
Write $A=(\gamma+1)/2$. Substituting $x=(c_0-a)t$ into the $C_+$ characteristic equation gives
$$
at=c_0s+At,s^\alpha
-\left(A-\frac1{1+\alpha}\right)s^{1+\alpha}.
$$
For $0<a<A$ and $0<\alpha\ll1$, the relevant emission time is exponentially small. The terms containing an explicit factor $s$ are therefore beyond all algebraic orders, leaving
$$
s^\alpha\sim\frac aA.
$$
Thus the <singular small-power piston emission time> is
$$
\boxed{s(x,t)\sim
\left(\frac aA\right)^{1/\alpha}
=\left(\frac{2a}{\gamma+1}\right)^{1/\alpha}.}
$$
Although $s\to0$, the limiting state is nontrivial:
$$
u\sim-\frac{2a}{\gamma+1},
\qquad
c\sim c_0-\frac{\gamma-1}{\gamma+1}a,
$$
which is the centered-rarefaction state on the ray $x/t=c_0-a$ when $\alpha=0$.

The curve $C$ has dimension one. Its tangent has dimension one at every point except the origin and dimension two at the origin. By the <smooth locus of a variety>,
$$
\boxed{C_{\mathrm{sm}}=C\setminus\{(0,0,0)\}},
$$
and the origin is its unique <singular point of an algebraic variety>. Each reduced point component has dimension zero and zero-dimensional tangent space, so both $P$ and $Q$ are smooth on their respective components.

The equations $3m=4l$ and $2n=5k$ are equivalent to
$$
m=4r,
\quad l=3r,
\qquad
n=5s,
\quad k=2s
$$
for nonnegative integers $r,s$. Hence the language is
$$
\{a^{4r}b^{5s}c^{2s}d^{3r}:r,s\geq0\},
$$
which is the <nested two-pair count language> generated by
$$
S\to aaaaSddd\mid T,
\qquad
T\to bbbbbTcc\mid\epsilon.
$$
It is therefore context-free.

