The <Householder-John theorem> states: if $A=M-N$ is <Hermitian matrix>[Hermitian] positive definite and $M^*+N$ is also Hermitian positive definite, then $M$ is invertible and
$$
\rho(M^{-1}N)<1.
$$

Now let $A=D+L+L^T$ be real symmetric positive definite and tridiagonal. For the Jacobi splitting, $M=D$ and $N=-(L+L^T)$. Positive definiteness of $A$ implies every diagonal entry is positive, so $D$ is positive definite. Introduce the alternating-sign <diagonal matrix>
$$
S=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,1,-1,\ldots).
$$
Tridiagonality gives
$$
SAS=D-L-L^T=M^*+N.
$$
Since $S$ is orthogonal, $SAS$ is positive definite whenever $A$ is. Both hypotheses of the Householder-John theorem hold, and hence the Jacobi iteration matrix satisfies $\rho(M^{-1}N)<1$. Therefore the <Jacobi convergence for a symmetric positive-definite tridiagonal matrix> follows.

Lower the free index of $K$. The <Killing equation> makes the last two indices of $\nabla_\nu\nabla_\mu K_\alpha$ antisymmetric after the appropriate derivative is moved. Combining the three resulting <Ricci identities> cyclically gives
$$
\nabla_\nu\nabla_\mu K_\alpha
=\frac12\left(
[\nabla_\nu,\nabla_\mu]K_\alpha
-[\nabla_\mu,\nabla_\alpha]K_\nu
+[\nabla_\alpha,\nabla_\nu]K_\mu
\right).
$$
Applying the covector form of the Ricci identity and then the <first Bianchi identity> reduces this to
$$
\nabla_\nu\nabla_\mu K_\alpha
=R_{\alpha\mu\nu\beta}K^\beta.
$$
Raising $\alpha$ proves the <second covariant derivative of a Killing vector> identity
$$
\boxed{\nabla_\nu\nabla_\mu K^\alpha
=R^\alpha{}_{\mu\nu\beta}K^\beta.}
$$
Contracting $\mu$ and $\nu$ and using the definition and symmetries of the <Ricci tensor> gives
$$
\boxed{\nabla_\mu\nabla^\mu K^\alpha
=-R^\alpha{}_{\beta}K^\beta.}
$$

Take <context-free grammar>[context-free grammars] $G_L,G_M$ for $L,M$ and rename their nonterminals so that they are disjoint. If their start symbols are $S_L,S_M$, add a new start symbol $S$ and the production
$$
S\to S_LS_M.
$$
All productions remain context-free. A derivation from $S$ produces a word from $L$ followed by a word from $M$, and every such concatenation has a derivation. Thus the new grammar generates $LM$, proving <closure of context-free languages under concatenation>.

= 5J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5J}

An <exponential dispersion family> has densities of the form
$$
f(y;\theta,\phi)
=a(y,\phi)\exp\left\{\frac{y\theta-b(\theta)}{\phi}\right\},
$$
where $\theta$ is the <natural parameter of an exponential family>[canonical parameter], $\phi>0$ is the <dispersion parameter>, and the support does not depend on $\theta$. Its mean and variance are
$$
\mathbb E Y=b'(\theta),
\qquad
\operatorname{var}(Y)=\phi b''(\theta).
$$

For the given <Gamma distribution>, set
$$
\phi=\frac1\alpha,
\qquad
\theta=-\frac\lambda\alpha<0,
\qquad
b(\theta)=-\log(-\theta).
$$
Then
$$
a(y,\phi)
=\frac{y^{1/\phi-1}}{\Gamma(1/\phi)\phi^{1/\phi}}
$$
gives
$$
a(y,\phi)
\exp\left\{\frac{y\theta-b(\theta)}\phi\right\}
=\frac{\lambda^\alpha}{\Gamma(\alpha)}y^{\alpha-1}e^{-\lambda y}.
$$
Thus the gamma laws form the <Gamma exponential dispersion family>.

Directly integrating, or using the <cumulant-generating function of a gamma distribution>,
$$
M_Y(t)=\left(\frac\lambda{\lambda-t}\right)^\alpha,
\qquad t<\lambda,
$$
so
$$
K_Y(t)=\log M_Y(t)
=-\alpha\log\left(1-\frac t\lambda\right).
$$
Differentiation at zero yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E Y=K_Y'(0)=\frac\alpha\lambda},
\qquad
\boxed{\operatorname{var}(Y)=K_Y''(0)=\frac\alpha{\lambda^2}}.
$$

= 6C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6C}

In this discrete <age-structured population model>, individuals of age $a\geq1$ present during the breeding season each produce $b_a$ newborns, so the number entering age zero is
$$
n_{0,t}=\sum_{a=1}^{\infty}n_{a,t}b_a.
$$
An age-$a$ individual survives the following winter with probability $1-\mu_a$ and is then age $a+1$ in the next year, giving
$$
n_{a+1,t+1}=(1-\mu_a)n_{a,t}.
$$
Together with the initial age distribution, these equations determine every later cohort.

Substitute $n_{a,t}=r_a\gamma^t$ into the survival equation. Then
$$
r_{a+1}=\frac{1-\mu_a}{\gamma}r_a,
$$
and iteration gives
$$
r_a=r_0\gamma^{-a}\prod_{i=0}^{a-1}(1-\mu_i).
$$
The birth equation becomes
$$
r_0=\sum_{a=1}^{\infty}r_ab_a
=r_0\sum_{a=1}^{\infty}
\left[\prod_{i=0}^{a-1}(1-\mu_i)\right]\gamma^{-a}b_a.
$$
A nonzero population mode therefore requires the <discrete Euler-Lotka equation>
$$
\boxed{\phi(\gamma)=1},
\qquad
\phi(\gamma)=\sum_{a=1}^{\infty}
\left[\prod_{i=0}^{a-1}(1-\mu_i)\right]\gamma^{-a}b_a.
$$

For nonnegative vital rates with at least one surviving reproductive age and enough decay to make the series finite, $\phi$ is continuous and strictly decreasing on $\gamma>0$, tends to infinity as $\gamma\downarrow0$, and tends to zero as $\gamma\to\infty$. The <intermediate value theorem> gives one positive solution, and strict monotonicity makes it unique.

The value $\phi(1)$ is the expected lifetime offspring count under these fixed rates. Since $\phi$ decreases, $\phi(1)>1$ places the root at $\gamma>1$ and the population grows geometrically; $\phi(1)<1$ places it at $\gamma<1$ and the population shrinks. Equality gives replacement, $\gamma=1$.

= 7A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With $u=x^4$, the <change of variables formula> gives
$$
\int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^4}}
=\frac14\int_0^1u^{-3/4}(1-u)^{-1/2}\,du
=\frac14B\left(\frac14,\frac12\right).
$$
The <beta--gamma identity>, $\Gamma(1/2)=\sqrt\pi$, and the <Gamma reflection formula> give
$$
\frac14B\left(\frac14,\frac12\right)
=\frac{\Gamma(1/4)\sqrt\pi}{4\Gamma(3/4)},
\qquad
\Gamma(1/4)\Gamma(3/4)=\pi\sqrt2.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^4}}
=\frac{\Gamma(1/4)^2}{\sqrt{32\pi}}}.
$$

= b
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the suggested substitution
$$
x=\frac{t}{\sqrt{2-t^2}}.
$$
It maps $[0,1]$ to itself and satisfies
$$
dx=\frac{2\,dt}{(2-t^2)^{3/2}},
\qquad
1-x^4=\frac{4(1-t^2)}{(2-t^2)^2}.
$$
Hence
$$
\int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^4}}
=\frac1{\sqrt2}\int_0^1
\frac{dt}{\sqrt{(1-t^2)(1-t^2/2)}}
=\frac1{\sqrt2}K(1/\sqrt2),
$$
where $K$ is the <complete elliptic integral of the first kind>. Part (a) and the <Gamma function recurrence> $\Gamma(5/4)=\Gamma(1/4)/4$ now give
$$
\boxed{K(1/\sqrt2)
=\frac{\Gamma(1/4)^2}{4\sqrt\pi}
=\frac{4\Gamma(5/4)^2}{\sqrt\pi}}.
$$

= 8E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=8e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let
$$
\xi_i(t)=\left.\frac{\partial Q_i(s,t)}{\partial s}\right|_{s=0}
$$
be the infinitesimal generator of the continuous symmetry, and let
$$
p_i=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}
$$
be the <canonical momentum>. <Noether theorem> states that along every trajectory satisfying the <Euler-Lagrange equations>,
$$
\boxed{J=\sum_{i=1}^np_i\xi_i}
$$
is conserved.

Indeed, differentiate the invariance of $L$ with respect to $s$ at zero:
$$
0=\sum_i\left(
\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}\xi_i
+\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\dot\xi_i
\right).
$$
On an Euler-Lagrange trajectory, $\partial L/\partial q_i=d p_i/dt$, so
$$
0=\sum_i(\dot p_i\xi_i+p_i\dot\xi_i)
=\frac d{dt}\sum_i p_i\xi_i.
$$
This proves the <Noether conserved quantity for a mechanical point symmetry>.

= b
{parent=8e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In <cylindrical coordinates>,
$$
L=\frac m2(\dot r^2+r^2\dot\phi^2+\dot z^2)-f(r,\phi-kz).
$$
The screw transformation
$$
z\mapsto z+s,
\qquad
\phi\mapsto\phi+ks
$$
leaves $r$ and $\phi-kz$ fixed, so it is the <helical symmetry of a potential>. Its infinitesimal generator has $\xi_z=1$ and $\xi_\phi=k$. Noether's theorem therefore gives the conserved quantity
$$
\boxed{p_z+kp_\phi
=m\dot z+kmr^2\dot\phi}.
$$
This is a linear combination of axial <linear momentum> and axial <angular momentum>.

= 9B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=9B}

For an infinitesimal test mass $\delta m$ on the surface of a homogeneous sphere, its Newtonian <energy>[mechanical energy] is
$$
E=\frac12\delta m\,\dot R^2-\frac{GM\delta m}{R}.
$$
The spatially flat case corresponds to zero specific energy. Using
$$
M=\frac{4\pi}{3}\rho R^3
$$
and dividing by $\delta mR^2/2$ gives
$$
\left(\frac{\dot R}{R}\right)^2=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho.
$$
Since $R$ is proportional to the <scale factor> $a$, this is the <Newtonian derivation of the flat Friedmann equation>
$$
\boxed{H^2=\left(\frac{\dot a}{a}\right)^2=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho}.
$$

Now take $a(t_0)=1$ and $\rho=\rho_0a^{-4}$. Put
$$
C=\sqrt{\frac{8\pi G\rho_0}{3}}.
$$
The contracting branch of the <Friedmann equation> is
$$
\frac{\dot a}{a}=-Ca^{-2},
\qquad
\dot a=-\frac Ca.
$$
Integrating $a\,da=-C\,dt$ from $t_0$ gives
$$
\frac12(a(t)^2-1)=-C(t-t_0).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{a(t)=\sqrt{1-2C(t-t_0)}}
$$
on the contracting interval, reaching $a=0$ at $t=t_0+1/(2C)$.

= 10D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Information and Computation}

= a
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $|\alpha\rangle=a|0\rangle+b|1\rangle$. Expanding in the orthonormal <Bell state>[Bell basis] of systems $CA$ gives the <Bell-basis teleportation identity>
$$
|\alpha\rangle_C|\beta_{00}\rangle_{AB}
=\frac12\bigl(
|\beta_{00}\rangle_{CA}|\alpha\rangle_B
+|\beta_{01}\rangle_{CA}Z|\alpha\rangle_B
+|\beta_{10}\rangle_{CA}X|\alpha\rangle_B
+|\beta_{11}\rangle_{CA}XZ|\alpha\rangle_B
\bigr).
$$
Thus, uniformly for $x,z\in\{0,1\}$,
$$
\boxed{|\mu_{xz}\rangle=X^xZ^z|\alpha\rangle}.
$$
The states are unique because the four Bell states are an <orthonormal basis> of the two-qubit space. In particular,
$$
\boxed{|\mu_{10}\rangle=X|\alpha\rangle}.
$$

= b
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Alice performs a projective <Bell-basis measurement> on her qubits $C$ and $A$. Outcome $(x,z)$ occurs with probability $1/4$ and leaves Bob's qubit in
$$
X^xZ^z|\alpha\rangle.
$$
Alice sends the two classical bits $(x,z)$ to Bob. He applies the inverse Pauli operation
$$
(X^xZ^z)^{-1}=Z^zX^x,
$$
recovering $|\alpha\rangle$ exactly, up to an irrelevant <global phase>. This is <quantum teleportation>: only <local operations and classical communication> are used, while the shared Bell pair supplies the entanglement resource.

= c
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

No. Before any operation, Bob's <reduced density matrix> is $I/2$, independent of $|\alpha\rangle$. Any trace-preserving operation performed only by Alice leaves Bob's reduced state unchanged when her uncommunicated outcomes are averaged. This is the <no-communication theorem>, or <quantum no-signalling>.

If Bob received the unknown state, his reduced state would instead be the pure state $|\alpha\rangle\langle\alpha|$, which depends on Alice's input. That would let Alice signal by choosing different inputs, contradicting no-signalling. The two classical outcome bits are therefore essential.

= 11G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

A binary <linear code> of length $n$ and rank $k$ is a $k$-dimensional vector subspace $C\leq\mathbb F_2^n$. Its minimum distance $d$ is the least <Hamming weight> of a nonzero codeword, equivalently the least <Hamming distance> between distinct codewords.

= a
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Delete any $d-1$ coordinates. The resulting puncturing map
$$
\pi:C\longrightarrow\mathbb F_2^{n-d+1}
$$
is injective: if $\pi(x)=\pi(y)$ for distinct codewords, then the nonzero codeword $x-y$ is supported on at most $d-1$ coordinates, contradicting the minimum distance. Thus
$$
2^k=|C|\leq2^{n-d+1},
$$
which is the <Singleton bound>
$$
\boxed{n\geq d+k-1}.
$$

= b
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $S$ be the support of the weight-$d$ codeword $x$, and let $\pi_S$ delete all coordinates in $S$. Its kernel consists of codewords supported in $S$. If $0\ne y\in\ker\pi_S$, then $\operatorname{wt}(y)\geq d$ but $|S|=d$, so $y$ is nonzero in every coordinate of $S$. Over $\mathbb F_2$ this forces $y=x$. Hence
$$
\ker\pi_S=\langle x\rangle
$$
has dimension one, and the <punctured code> $C'=\pi_S(C)$ has length $n-d$ and rank $k-1$ by the <rank-nullity theorem>.

To bound its minimum distance, take $y\in C$ whose puncture $y'$ is nonzero. If $y$ has $r$ nonzero coordinates in $S$, then $y+x$ has $d-r$ there, while both words have the same $\operatorname{wt}(y')$ nonzero coordinates outside $S$. Both $y$ and $y+x$ are nonzero codewords, so
$$
\operatorname{wt}(y')+r\geq d,
\qquad
\operatorname{wt}(y')+d-r\geq d.
$$
Adding gives $2\operatorname{wt}(y')\geq d$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{d'\geq\left\lceil\frac d2\right\rceil}.
$$

= c
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply part (b) repeatedly. After the first puncturing, the code has rank $k-1$, length $n-d$, and minimum distance at least $\lceil d/2\rceil$. Repeating with a minimum-weight codeword at each stage gives
$$
n\geq d+\left\lceil\frac d2\right\rceil
+\left\lceil\frac d{2^2}\right\rceil+cdots
+\left\lceil\frac d{2^{k-1}}\right\rceil,
$$
because nested ceilings obey
$$
\left\lceil\frac{\lceil d/2^j\rceil}{2}\right\rceil
=\left\lceil\frac d{2^{j+1}}\right\rceil.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{n\geq d+\sum_{1\leq l\leq k-1}
\left\lceil\frac d{2^l}\right\rceil},
$$
which is the binary <Griesmer bound>.

= 12H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= Solution
{parent=12H}

Let $\widehat\delta$ be the <extended transition function of a deterministic finite automaton>. States $p,q\in Q$ are equivalent, or indistinguishable, when
$$
p\sim q
\iff
\bigl(\widehat\delta(p,w)\in F
\Longleftrightarrow
\widehat\delta(q,w)\in F\bigr)
\quad\text{for every }w\in\Sigma^*.
$$
The <quotient deterministic finite automaton by indistinguishable states> $D/{\sim}$ has state set $Q/{\sim}$, initial state $[q_0]$, accepting states $\{[q]:q\in F\}$, and transition
$$
\overline\delta([q],a)=[\delta(q,a)].
$$
Right invariance of $\sim$ makes this well-defined, and the quotient accepts $L(D)$.

To prove minimality, choose for each state $q$ of the accessible automaton $D$ a word $w_q$ with $\widehat\delta_D(q_0,w_q)=q$. Run the same word in any DFA $A$ accepting $L(D)$, and assign the class $[q]$ the reached state of $A$. If two distinct classes $[p]\ne[q]$ reached the same state of $A$, every continuation $u$ would have the same acceptance result after $w_p$ and $w_q$. Since $A$ and $D$ accept the same language, this would make $p$ and $q$ indistinguishable in $D$, a contradiction. Thus $A$ has at least one distinct state for every class in $D/{\sim}$, proving that the quotient is the <minimal deterministic finite automaton>.

For divisibility by seven, take states $Q=\{0,1,\ldots,6\}$, initial state $0$, accepting set $F=\{0\}$, and transitions
$$
\boxed{\delta(r,b)=2r+b\pmod7},
\qquad b\in\{0,1\}.
$$
After reading a word, the state is its binary value modulo seven, so this <binary divisibility automaton> accepts exactly the multiples of seven, with leading zeros allowed. Every state is accessible: the three-bit words representing $0,1,\ldots,6$ reach the corresponding residues.

For distinct residues $p,q$, let $w_p$ be the three-bit representation of $-p\pmod7$, chosen in $\{0,\ldots,6\}$. Since $2^3\equiv1\pmod7$, reading $w_p$ from state $p$ ends at
$$
2^3p+(-p)=0\pmod7,
$$
whereas reading it from $q$ ends at $q-p\ne0$. Thus $w_p$ distinguishes $p$ from $q$. All seven states are pairwise distinguishable, so the DFA is minimal by the <Myhill-Nerode theorem>.

= 13J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= a
{parent=13j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $Y_{ij}$ be the number of ice creams in price category $i\in\{\mathrm{high},\mathrm{low},\mathrm{medium}\}$ and score category $j\in\{\mathrm{excellent},\mathrm{good},\mathrm{poor}\}$. The fitted <Poisson regression> assumes that the nine counts are <independent random variables> with
$$
Y_{ij}\sim\operatorname{Pois}(\mu_{ij}),
\qquad
\log\mu_{ij}=\alpha_i+\beta_j,
$$
where the <logarithmic link function> is used and excellent is the <reference level in a regression factor>, so $\beta_{\mathrm{excellent}}=0$. Equivalently,
$$
\mu_{ij}=r_i s_j,
\qquad r_i=e^{\alpha_i},\quad s_j=e^{\beta_j}.
$$
This is the <independence log-linear model for a two-way contingency table>: price and score have main effects but no interaction.

= b
{parent=13j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $y_{i+}=\sum_jy_{ij}$ and $\mu_{i+}=\sum_j\mu_{ij}$. Under the model in part (a),
$$
\mu_{i+}=e^{\alpha_i}\sum_je^{\beta_j},
\qquad
p_j:=\frac{\mu_{ij}}{\mu_{i+}}
=\frac{e^{\beta_j}}{\sum_ke^{\beta_k}},
$$
and $p_j$ is independent of the price category $i$. The <Poisson-multinomial conditioning> identity gives
$$
(Y_{i1},Y_{i2},Y_{i3})\mid Y_{i+}=y_{i+}
\sim\operatorname{Multinomial}(y_{i+};p_1,p_2,p_3).
$$
More explicitly, the <likelihood function> for row $i$ factors as
$$
\prod_j\frac{e^{-\mu_{ij}}\mu_{ij}^{y_{ij}}}{y_{ij}!}
=
\frac{e^{-\mu_{i+}}\mu_{i+}^{y_{i+}}}{y_{i+}!}
\frac{y_{i+}!}{\prod_jy_{ij}!}\prod_jp_j^{y_{ij}}.
$$
The first factor is the likelihood of the row total and the second is the conditional <multinomial likelihood>. The free price effect $\alpha_i$ makes the first factor maximal at $\widehat\mu_{i+}=y_{i+}$, while maximizing the remaining factors gives
$$
\widehat p_j=\frac{y_{+j}}{y_{++}}.
$$
Consequently both fits have the same <maximum-likelihood fitted value>
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu_{ij}=y_{i+}\widehat p_j
=\frac{y_{i+}y_{+j}}{y_{++}}}.
$$
Here every row total is $30$, the column totals are $31,28,31$, and $y_{++}=90$, so every fitted row is
$$
\boxed{(31/3,\ 28/3,\ 31/3)}.
$$
This factorization is the <Poisson trick>.

= c
{parent=13j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <null hypothesis> is that price and score are independent, equivalently that the <independence log-linear model for a two-way contingency table> is correct. Against the saturated alternative, the reported <Poisson deviance> is the <likelihood-ratio test statistic>
$$
G^2=4.6135.
$$
Under the null and the usual large-sample regularity assumptions, <Wilks theorem> gives
$$
G^2\xrightarrow{\mathrm d}\chi^2_{(3-1)(3-1)}=\chi^2_4.
$$
The observations must be independent, the cell probabilities must not lie on the boundary of the parameter space, and the expected counts must be large enough for the <chi-squared asymptotic approximation>. The fitted counts are either $31/3$ or $28/3$, so the customary expected-count check is comfortably satisfied. Since
$$
4.6135<\chi^2_{4,0.99}\simeq13.277
$$
(equivalently, the <p-value> is about $0.329$), we do not reject the null at the $1\%$ <significance level>. The data provide no significant lack of fit for independence.

= d
{parent=13j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <Killing equation> gives $\nabla_\mu K^\mu=0$ and $\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}=\nabla_\mu K_\lambda$. Commuting derivatives once and contracting the <Ricci identity> gives
$$
\nabla^\lambda\nabla_\mu K_\lambda=R_{\mu\beta}K^\beta.
$$
Therefore, using the <contracted Bianchi identity> and symmetry of $R_{\mu\beta}$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
2\nabla^\mu\nabla^\lambda\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}
&=2\nabla^\mu(R_{\mu\beta}K^\beta)\\
&=K^\beta\nabla_\beta R
+2R_{\mu\beta}\nabla^\mu K^\beta\\
&=K^\beta\nabla_\beta R.
\end{aligned}
$$
Since $\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}$ is antisymmetric, swapping the two contracted index names shows that the left side equals its derivative-antisymmetrized form:
$$
\boxed{K^\alpha\nabla_\alpha R
=2\nabla^{[\mu}\nabla^{\lambda]}\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}.}
$$
Now set $A_{\mu\lambda}=\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}$. The right side is the contracted commutator $[\nabla^\mu,\nabla^\lambda]A_{\mu\lambda}$. By the <curvature commutator on a covariant tensor>, it is a sum of contractions of the symmetric <Ricci tensor> with the antisymmetric tensor $A$, and hence vanishes. Thus
$$
\boxed{K^\alpha\nabla_\alpha R=0,}
$$
so the <Killing vector preserves scalar curvature>.

Both variables are <ordinal categorical variables>, and the proposed alternative is directional: higher scores should tend to occur at higher prices. Assign increasing numerical scores to low, medium, high price and to poor, good, excellent quality, and use a one-sided <linear-by-linear association test>. Its statistic is based on the sample covariance, or equivalently the correlation, of those scores under the fixed margins.

This test uses one degree of freedom and directs its rejection region toward positive association. The four-degree-of-freedom <likelihood-ratio test of independence in a contingency table> spends power on every possible departure from independence, including unordered and negative patterns, so the ordered one-sided test is more powerful when the stated alternative is credible.

= 14A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Papperitz symbol>
$$
P\left\{
\begin{matrix}
a&b&c\\
\alpha&\beta&\gamma\\
\alpha'&\beta'&\gamma'
\end{matrix};z
\right\}
$$
represents the two-dimensional <solution space of a homogeneous linear differential equation> for a second-order <Fuchsian differential equation> on the <Riemann sphere>. Its only singularities are the three <regular singular points> $a,b,c$. The two entries beneath each point are its <characteristic exponents at a regular singular point>: for example, in the nonresonant case a local basis near $a$ has leading behaviours $(z-a)^\alpha$ and $(z-a)^{\alpha'}$. If one of the points is infinity, exponent $\rho$ conventionally means behaviour $z^{-\rho}$ there.

The six exponents obey the <Fuchs relation>
$$
\boxed{\alpha+\alpha'+\beta+\beta'+\gamma+\gamma'=1}.
$$
Subject to this relation, the symbol determines the corresponding Riemann differential equation; resonant differences between exponents may produce <logarithmic Frobenius solutions> without changing the exponent data recorded by the symbol.

= b
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\omega$ solve the equation represented by the original symbol and define a <dependent-variable rescaling of a Papperitz symbol>
$$
\omega_1(z)=\left(\frac{z-a}{z-b}\right)^\delta\omega(z),
$$
with a consistent local choice of branch. Near $a$, the multiplier is a nonzero analytic factor times $(z-a)^\delta$, so both exponents at $a$ increase by $\delta$. Near $b$, it is a nonzero analytic factor times $(z-b)^{-\delta}$, so both exponents at $b$ decrease by $\delta$. It is analytic and nonzero near $c$, so the exponents there are unchanged. Therefore the transformed equation has symbol
$$
\boxed{
P\left\{
\begin{matrix}
a&b&c\\
\alpha+\delta&\beta-\delta&\gamma\\
\alpha'+\delta&\beta'-\delta&\gamma'
\end{matrix};z
\right\}}.
$$
The opposite shifts also preserve the <Fuchs relation>.

= c
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <Möbius transformation of a Papperitz symbol> changes the independent variable by a <Möbius transformation>. In particular,
$$
u=\frac{(b-c)(z-a)}{(b-a)(z-c)}
$$
maps $a,b,c$ to $0,1,\infty$, respectively. Because a Möbius map is locally biholomorphic away from its pole, it carries each exponent pair with its singular point and gives
$$
P\left\{
\begin{matrix}
0&1&\infty\\
\alpha&\beta&\gamma\\
\alpha'&\beta'&\gamma'
\end{matrix};u
\right\}.
$$
Now define a <dependent-variable rescaling of a Papperitz symbol> by writing the old dependent variable as
$$
y(u)=u^\alpha(1-u)^\beta v(u),
$$
so that $v=u^{-\alpha}(1-u)^{-\beta}y$. This <dependent-variable rescaling of a Papperitz symbol> subtracts $\alpha$ from both exponents at zero, subtracts $\beta$ from both exponents at one, and adds $\alpha+\beta$ to both exponents at infinity. Hence $v$ has symbol
$$
P\left\{
\begin{matrix}
0&1&\infty\\
0&0&\gamma+\alpha+\beta\\
\alpha'-\alpha&\beta'-\beta&\gamma'+\alpha+\beta
\end{matrix};u
\right\}.
$$
Define
$$
A=\gamma+\alpha+\beta,
\qquad
B=\gamma'+\alpha+\beta,
\qquad
C=1+\alpha-\alpha'.
$$
The Fuchs relation gives $\alpha'-\alpha=1-C$ and $\beta'-\beta=C-A-B$. After renaming $u$ as $z$, the symbol is therefore
$$
\boxed{
P\left\{
\begin{matrix}
0&1&\infty\\
0&0&A\\
1-C&C-A-B&B
\end{matrix};z
\right\}},
$$
which is the <Gauss hypergeometric equation>. Renaming $A,B,C$ as $a,b,c$ gives exactly the displayed symbol in the question.

= d
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the hypergeometric symbol, select the exponent $1-c$ at zero and factor out its local power by writing
$$
y(z)=z^{1-c}v(z).
$$
The <exponential shifting of a Papperitz symbol> makes the exponents of $v$ equal to $0,c-1$ at zero, $0,c-a-b$ at one, and $a-c+1,b-c+1$ at infinity. These are the hypergeometric parameters
$$
A=a-c+1,
\qquad
B=b-c+1,
\qquad
C=2-c,
$$
because $1-C=c-1$ and $C-A-B=c-a-b$. The solution for $v$ that is analytic at zero and equals one there is therefore the <Gauss hypergeometric function> $F(A,B,C;z)$. Thus the <second local hypergeometric solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(z)=z^{1-c}F(a-c+1,b-c+1,2-c;z)}.
$$
For noninteger $c$ this is linearly independent from $F(a,b,c;z)$; at resonant values, the usual limiting or logarithmic modification may be required.

= 15B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Since $H=\dot a/a$, differentiation gives the <Hubble parameter identity>
$$
\dot H=\frac{\ddot a}{a}-H^2.
$$
Subtracting the given <Friedmann equation> from the <Raychaudhuri equation> therefore yields
$$
\dot H
=-\frac{8\pi G}{3}(\dot\phi^2-V)
-\frac{8\pi G}{3}\left(\frac12\dot\phi^2+V\right)
=-4\pi G\dot\phi^2.
$$
On the other hand, differentiating the Friedmann equation gives
$$
2H\dot H
=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\dot\phi(\ddot\phi+V'(\phi)).
$$
Substitute $\dot H=-4\pi G\dot\phi^2$ and divide by $(8\pi G/3)\dot\phi$, which is permitted by the assumption $\dot\phi\ne0$. The result is the homogeneous <inflaton equation of motion>
$$
\boxed{\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0}.
$$
The middle term is <Hubble friction>.

= b
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4$, the <slow-roll approximation> gives
$$
H^2\simeq\frac{8\pi G}{3}\lambda\phi^4,
\qquad
3H\dot\phi\simeq-4\lambda\phi^3.
$$
Taking the expanding branch $H>0$ and using $H=\sqrt{8\pi G\lambda/3}\,\phi^2$ gives the <first-order linear differential equation>
$$
\dot\phi=-\sqrt{\frac{2\lambda}{3\pi G}}\,\phi.
$$
Thus the <quartic-potential slow-roll solution> with the normalization used in this question is
$$
\boxed{
\phi(t)=\phi_0
\exp\left[-\sqrt{\frac{2\lambda}{3\pi G}}(t-t_0)\right]}.
$$
If $\phi_0<0$, the field remains negative and $\dot\phi>0$, so it rolls toward the potential minimum at zero.

= c
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <number of e-folds> $N=\log a$ satisfies
$$
dN=d\log a=H\,dt.
$$
When $\phi$ is monotone, the <chain rule> gives $dt=d\phi/\dot\phi$, and hence
$$
dN=\frac{H}{\dot\phi}\,d\phi.
$$
Using the two <slow-roll equations>, $3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'(\phi)$ and $H^2\simeq8\pi GV/3$, eliminates both $H$ and time:
$$
\boxed{dN\simeq-8\pi G\frac{V(\phi)}{V'(\phi)}\,d\phi}.
$$
This is the differential form of the <slow-roll e-fold count>.

= d
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the quartic potential,
$$
\frac{V}{V'}=\frac{\phi}{4}.
$$
Integrating the result of part (c) along the trajectory from $\phi_i$ to $\phi_f$ gives
$$
N\simeq-8\pi G\int_{\phi_i}^{\phi_f}\frac{\phi}{4}\,d\phi
=\boxed{\pi G(\phi_i^2-\phi_f^2)}.
$$
Because the negative field rolls toward zero, $\phi_i<\phi_f<0$ and $|\phi_i|>|\phi_f|$, so this <monomial slow-roll e-fold count> is positive.

= e
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

In the decelerating <hot Big Bang model>, the <particle horizon> at recombination encloses only a finite comoving region. Widely separated parts of the <cosmic microwave background> therefore had no overlapping past light cones in that model, although they have almost the same temperature. This is the <horizon problem>. During <cosmic inflation>, accelerated expansion makes the <comoving Hubble radius> $(aH)^{-1}$ shrink. A small region that was initially in causal contact can then expand to contain the whole presently observable universe.

The <flatness problem> follows from
$$
|\Omega-1|=\frac{|k|}{a^2H^2}.
$$
During ordinary radiation- or matter-dominated decelerating expansion this quantity grows, so the observed closeness of the <cosmological density parameter> $\Omega$ to one requires extreme tuning at early times. Inflation makes $aH$ grow nearly exponentially and therefore drives $|\Omega-1|$ rapidly toward zero. A sufficiently large <number of e-folds> makes spatial flatness a dynamical outcome rather than a finely tuned initial condition.

= 16I
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16I}

The <completeness theorem for propositional logic> states that for every set of formulae $\Gamma$ and formula $\varphi$,
$$
\boxed{\Gamma\models\varphi\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\Gamma\vdash\varphi}.
$$
With the <soundness theorem for propositional logic>, this is an equivalence. It is also equivalent to saying that every <consistent set of formulae> has a model.

The usual countable proof lists all formulae and extends a consistent theory one formula at a time. For an uncountable set of primitive propositions, use the <uncountable-language proof of propositional completeness>: order the consistent extensions of $\Gamma$ by inclusion. The union of every chain is consistent, since a proof of a contradiction would use only finitely many assumptions and hence would already lie in one member of the chain. <Zorn lemma> gives a <maximal consistent set in propositional logic> $M$. Define a <Boolean valuation> by $v(p)=1$ exactly when $p\in M$. The usual <structural induction> on formulae proves the truth lemma
$$
v(\theta)=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\theta\in M,
$$
so $v$ is a model of $\Gamma$.

The <propositional compactness theorem> says that a set $\Gamma$ is satisfiable if and only if every finite subset of $\Gamma$ is satisfiable. Only the forward implication is immediate. For the converse, if $\Gamma$ had no model, then $\Gamma\models\bot$, so completeness would give $\Gamma\vdash\bot$. Every <formal proof> uses finitely many assumptions, so some finite $\Gamma_0\subseteq\Gamma$ would prove $\bot$ and, by soundness, would have no model, a contradiction.

The <decidability theorem for propositional logic> says that there is an algorithm deciding whether a propositional formula is a theorem. A formula contains only finitely many primitive propositions, even when the full language is uncountable. Its finite <truth table> decides whether it is valid, and completeness says that validity is equivalent to theoremhood.

Now let $(X,<)$ be the given <partially ordered set>. For every distinct $x,y\in X$, introduce propositions $p_{xy}$ and $q_{xy}$. Form a propositional theory $T$ containing the following clauses:

* $p_{xy}\leftrightarrow\neg p_{yx}$ and $q_{xy}\leftrightarrow\neg q_{yx}$ for distinct $x,y$;
* $(p_{xy}\wedge p_{yz})\to p_{xz}$ and $(q_{xy}\wedge q_{yz})\to q_{xz}$ for pairwise distinct $x,y,z$;
* $p_{xy}$ and $q_{xy}$ whenever $x<y$ in the original order;
* $\neg(p_{xy}\wedge q_{xy})$ and $\neg(p_{yx}\wedge q_{yx})$ whenever $x$ and $y$ are incomparable in the original order.

The first two families say that $p$ and $q$ encode <strict total orders>. The third makes both orders extend $<$, while the fourth makes them disagree on every incomparable pair.

Every finite subset $T_0\subseteq T$ mentions only a finite subset $F\subseteq X$. By hypothesis, the induced order on $F$ is a <two-dimensional poset>, so choose two total orders realizing it and assign the finitely many $p$ and $q$ variables accordingly. This satisfies $T_0$. Thus every finite subset of $T$ is satisfiable, and propositional compactness gives a model of all of $T$.

Define $x<_1y$ when $p_{xy}$ is true and $x<_2y$ when $q_{xy}$ is true. The clauses make $<_1$ and $<_2$ total orders on $X$. They both contain the original order, and their intersection contains no incomparable pair. Hence
$$
x<y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x<_1y\text{ and }x<_2y,
$$
which proves the <finite-local characterization of two-dimensional partially ordered sets>.

= 17G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= a
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $A$ be the <adjacency matrix of a graph>. Since $G$ is a <regular graph> of degree $d$, every row of $A$ sums to $d$. Therefore, for $\mathbf e=(1,\ldots,1)^T$,
$$
\boxed{A\mathbf e=d\mathbf e},
$$
so $d$ is a <graph eigenvalue>.

If $Av=dv$, then the quadratic form of the <graph Laplacian> $L=dI-A$ gives
$$
0=v^*(dI-A)v
=\sum_{\{i,j\}\in E(G)}|v_i-v_j|^2.
$$
Every summand is nonnegative, so $v_i=v_j$ along every edge. The graph is <connected graph>[connected], hence all coordinates of $v$ are equal and $v$ is a scalar multiple of $\mathbf e$. Thus the $d$-eigenspace is one-dimensional. Since $A$ is a <symmetric matrix>, the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> makes it diagonalizable, so the algebraic multiplicity is also one. This proves the <constant eigenvector of a regular graph> result.

= b
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Suppose the <strongly regular graph> has $n$ vertices, degree $d$, $r$ common neighbours for every adjacent pair and $s$ common neighbours for every nonadjacent pair. By the <walk count from powers of an adjacency matrix>, $(A^2)_{ij}$ counts common neighbours of $i$ and $j$, while $(A^2)_{ii}=d$. Hence the <adjacency-matrix relation for a strongly regular graph> is
$$
A^2=(d-s)I+(r-s)A+sJ,
$$
where $J$ is the <all-ones matrix>.

The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> gives an orthonormal eigenbasis for $A$. Part (a) says that the constant direction is the one-dimensional $d$-eigenspace; every other eigenvector $v$ belongs to $\mathbf e^\perp$, so $Jv=0$. If $Av=\theta v$, the displayed identity gives
$$
\theta^2-(r-s)\theta-(d-s)=0.
$$
There are at most two possible roots $\theta$ in addition to $d$. Thus $G$ has at most three distinct eigenvalues.

= c
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For an eigenvector $v$ with eigenvalue $\theta\in\{\lambda,\mu\}$,
$$
Bv=(A-\lambda I)(A-\mu I)v
=(\theta-\lambda)(\theta-\mu)v=0.
$$
By part (a), every vector orthogonal to $\mathbf e$ is a linear combination of such eigenvectors, so $B$ vanishes on $\mathbf e^\perp$. On the remaining eigenspace,
$$
B\mathbf e=(d-\lambda)(d-\mu)\mathbf e.
$$
The <orthogonal projection> onto $\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf e\}$ is $J/n$. It follows that
$$
\boxed{B=\frac{(d-\lambda)(d-\mu)}nJ}.
$$
Put $\kappa=(d-\lambda)(d-\mu)/n$. For $i\ne j$, the identity $B=\kappa J$ reads
$$
(A^2)_{ij}-(\lambda+\mu)A_{ij}=\kappa,
$$
and therefore
$$
(A^2)_{ij}=
\begin{cases}
\kappa+\lambda+\mu,&i\text{ and }j\text{ adjacent},\\
\kappa,&i\text{ and }j\text{ nonadjacent}.
\end{cases}
$$
Because $(A^2)_{ij}$ is the number of common neighbours, these two constants prove that $G$ is strongly regular. This is the converse half of the <three-eigenvalue characterization of a connected strongly regular graph>.

= d
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Suppose the only distinct eigenvalues are $d$ and $\theta$. As in part (c), $A$ acts by $d$ on $\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf e\}$ and by $\theta$ on $\mathbf e^\perp$, so its <spectral decomposition> is
$$
A=\theta I+\frac{d-\theta}{n}J.
$$
Every diagonal entry of an adjacency matrix is zero, giving
$$
\theta+\frac{d-\theta}{n}=0.
$$
Thus every off-diagonal entry is the same number $(d-\theta)/n=-\theta$. Since these entries belong to $\{0,1\}$ and the graph is connected with at least two vertices, that common value must be one. Hence $A=J-I$ and $G$ is the <complete graph> $K_n$.

Conversely, $K_n$ has adjacency matrix $J-I$, with eigenvalue $n-1$ on $\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf e\}$ and eigenvalue $-1$ on $\mathbf e^\perp$. Therefore the requested graphs are precisely
$$
\boxed{K_n\quad(n\geq2)},
$$
as stated by the <connected regular graph with two adjacency eigenvalues> characterization.

= 18F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= a
{parent=18f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Suppose otherwise and choose a relation
$$
\lambda_1\sigma_1(x)+\cdots+\lambda_m\sigma_m(x)=0
\qquad(x\in K)
$$
with the least possible number $m$ of nonzero coefficients. After relabelling and scaling, take every $\lambda_i\ne0$ and $\lambda_m=1$. Since the <field embeddings> $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_m$ are distinct, there is a $y\in K$ such that $\sigma_1(y)\ne\sigma_m(y)$.

Apply the relation to $xy$ and subtract $\sigma_m(y)$ times the relation at $x$. Because each <field embedding> is multiplicative, this gives
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}
\lambda_i\bigl(\sigma_i(y)-\sigma_m(y)\bigr)\sigma_i(x)=0
\qquad(x\in K).
$$
The $m$th term has disappeared, while the coefficient of $\sigma_1$ is nonzero. This is a shorter nontrivial relation, contradicting minimality. Hence distinct embeddings are linearly independent as functions. This is the <Artin independence theorem>.

= b
{parent=18f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

View every $k$-automorphism $\sigma_i:K\to K$ as an element of the $K$-vector space
$$
\operatorname{Hom}_k(K,K),
$$
where $K$ acts by multiplying output values. If $n=[K:k]$ and $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ is a $k$-basis of $K$, a $k$-linear map is determined freely by its $n$ values on that basis. Thus
$$
\dim_K\operatorname{Hom}_k(K,K)=n.
$$
Part (a) says that the $m$ distinct automorphisms are <linearly independent vectors> in this space, so
$$
\boxed{m\leq[K:k]}.
$$

Now let $k=K^G$, the <fixed field> of the finite automorphism group $G$. Its elements are fixed by every member of $G$, so the members of $G$ are distinct $K^G$-automorphisms of $K$. Applying the inequality gives the <automorphism-count bound for a finite field extension>
$$
\boxed{|G|\leq[K:K^G]}.
$$

= c
{parent=18f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The actions on the ordered pair $(x,y)$ satisfy
$$
\sigma^4=1,
\qquad
\tau^2=1,
\qquad
\tau\sigma\tau=\sigma^{-1}.
$$
Thus every element of $G$ has the form $\sigma^i$ or $\tau\sigma^i$ for $0\leq i<4$, so $|G|\leq8$. Their actions are
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
g&1&\sigma&\sigma^2&\sigma^3&\tau&\tau\sigma&\tau\sigma^2&\tau\sigma^3\\ \hline
(g(x),g(y))
&(x,y)&(y,-x)&(-x,-y)&(-y,x)
&(x,-y)&(-y,-x)&(-x,y)&(y,x).
\end{array}
$$
Because $x,y$ are <algebraically independent elements> over $\mathbb Q$, these eight substitutions are distinct. Hence
$$
\boxed{|G|=8},
$$
and the presentation identifies $G$ with the <dihedral group> $D_8$.

Put
$$
u=x^2+y^2,
\qquad
v=x^2y^2.
$$
Both generators $\sigma$ and $\tau$ fix $u$ and $v$, so for $F=\mathbb Q(u,v)$,
$$
F\subseteq K^G.
$$
The elements $x^2$ and $y^2$ are the two roots of
$$
T^2-uT+v,
$$
so $[F(x^2,y^2):F]\leq2$. Adjoining first $x$ and then $y$ takes at most two quadratic extensions, and the <tower law> gives
$$
[K:F]\leq2\cdot2\cdot2=8.
$$
Part (b) gives $8=|G|\leq[K:K^G]$. Since $F\subseteq K^G\subseteq K$, another use of the tower law gives
$$
8\leq[K:K^G]\leq[K:F]\leq8.
$$
All inequalities are equalities and $[K^G:F]=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{K^G=\mathbb Q(x^2+y^2,x^2y^2)},
$$
the <dihedral fixed field of a two-variable rational function field>.

= 19I
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= a
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Schur lemma> over $\mathbb C$ states that an <intertwining operator> $T:V\to W$ between finite-dimensional irreducible complex representations is either zero or an isomorphism. In particular, every intertwining endomorphism of $V$ is a scalar multiple of the identity.

Indeed, $\ker T\subseteq V$ and $\operatorname{im}T\subseteq W$ are <invariant subspaces>. If $T\ne0$, irreducibility gives $\ker T=0$ and $\operatorname{im}T=W$, so $T$ is an isomorphism. For an endomorphism $T:V\to V$, the <fundamental theorem of algebra> gives an eigenvalue $\lambda\in\mathbb C$. Then $T-\lambda I$ is an intertwiner with nonzero kernel, so the first part forces $T-\lambda I=0$. Hence $T=\lambda I$, completing the <proof of Schur lemma>.

= b
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $G=C_3=\langle g\rangle$ and let $e_0,e_1,e_2$ be the standard basis of its real <regular representation>, with $ge_j=e_{j+1}$ modulo three. Set
$$
U=\operatorname{span}\{e_0+e_1+e_2\},
\qquad
W=\{x_0e_0+x_1e_1+x_2e_2:x_0+x_1+x_2=0\}.
$$
Both are invariant, they are orthogonal, and
$$
\boxed{\mathbb RG=U\oplus W}.
$$
The line $U$ is the <trivial representation>. On the plane $W$, the generator acts as a rotation through $2\pi/3$. Such a rotation has no real eigenline, so $W$ has no nonzero proper invariant real subspace and is irreducible.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The two irreducible real modules $U$ and $W$ are nonisomorphic, so the kernel-image argument from part (a), which is valid over any field, gives
$$
\operatorname{Hom}_G(U,W)=0,
\qquad
\operatorname{Hom}_G(W,U)=0.
$$
Every endomorphism of the one-dimensional trivial module is scalar:
$$
\operatorname{End}_G(U)=\mathbb R.
$$

Identify the oriented plane $W$ with $\mathbb C$, so that $g$ acts by multiplication by $e^{2\pi i/3}$. A real-linear map commutes with this rotation exactly when it is multiplication by a complex number. Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{End}_G(W)
=\{aI+bJ:a,b\in\mathbb R,\ J^2=-I\}\cong\mathbb C},
$$
where $J$ is rotation through $\pi/2$. Taking $J$ gives a nonscalar intertwining endomorphism of an irreducible real representation. This is the <failure of the scalar conclusion of Schur lemma over the real numbers>.

= c
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $e_0,\ldots,e_{n-1}$ be the standard basis and let the generator $g$ send $e_j$ to $e_{j+1}$ modulo $n$. The constant vector
$$
c_0=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}e_j
$$
spans a trivial module. Since $n$ is even, the alternating vector
$$
c_{n/2}=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(-1)^je_j
$$
spans a sign module on which $g$ acts by $-1$.

For each $1\leq k<n/2$, define the real <discrete Fourier modes>
$$
c_k=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{2\pi kj}{n}\right)e_j,
\qquad
s_k=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi kj}{n}\right)e_j.
$$
The plane $W_k=\operatorname{span}\{c_k,s_k\}$ is invariant, and $g$ acts on it by rotation through $2\pi k/n$. Since this angle is neither zero nor $\pi$, $W_k$ is irreducible over $\mathbb R$. The traces $2\cos(2\pi k/n)$ are distinct for $1\leq k<n/2$, so these modules are pairwise nonisomorphic. Orthogonality of the <discrete Fourier transform> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb RC_n
=\langle c_0\rangle\oplus\langle c_{n/2}\rangle
\oplus\bigoplus_{k=1}^{(n-2)/2}W_k}.
$$
The dimensions sum to $1+1+2(n-2)/2=n$, so this is the whole regular module: two nonisomorphic one-dimensional summands and $(n-2)/2$ nonisomorphic two-dimensional summands.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

When $n$ is odd there is no alternating sign mode. The same cosine-sine construction gives invariant irreducible planes $W_k$ for
$$
1\leq k\leq\frac{n-1}{2},
$$
again pairwise nonisomorphic because their generator traces are distinct. The real Fourier decomposition is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb RC_n
=\langle c_0\rangle
\oplus\bigoplus_{k=1}^{(n-1)/2}W_k}.
$$
Its dimensions sum to $1+2(n-1)/2=n$. Thus the <real regular representation of a finite cyclic group> has one one-dimensional irreducible summand and $(n-1)/2$ nonisomorphic two-dimensional irreducible summands.

= 20G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= a
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <ring of integers of a quadratic field> has integral basis $1,\sqrt d$ when the square-free integer $d$ is not congruent to one modulo four. Since $d=2$,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]}.
$$
This is the <ring of integers of Q of square root two>.

= b
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Dirichlet unit theorem> states that for a <number field> with $r$ real embeddings and $s$ conjugate pairs of complex embeddings,
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times\cong\mu(K)\times\mathbb Z^{r+s-1},
$$
where $\mu(K)$ is its finite group of <roots of unity>. Here $K$ is real quadratic, so $(r,s)=(2,0)$ and $\mu(K)=\{\mathord\pm1\}$. Thus its unit group has rank one.

The element
$$
\varepsilon=1+\sqrt2
$$
is a unit because its <field norm> is
$$
N_{K/\mathbb Q}(\varepsilon)
=(1+\sqrt2)(1-\sqrt2)=-1.
$$
It is the smallest unit greater than one in the chosen real embedding. Indeed, write such a unit as $u=a+b\sqrt2$ and let $u'=a-b\sqrt2=N(u)/u$ be its <algebraic conjugate>. Since $|u'|<1<u$, both $a=(u+u')/2$ and $b=(u-u')/(2\sqrt2)$ are positive integers. Hence $a,b\geq1$ and $u\geq1+\sqrt2$. The rank-one logarithmic lattice in Dirichlet's theorem is therefore generated by $\log\varepsilon$. Consequently the <units of Q of square root two> are
$$
\boxed{\mathcal O_K^\times
=\{\mathord\pm(1+\sqrt2)^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}}.
$$

= c
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\varepsilon=1+\sqrt2$ and $P=(3+\sqrt2)$. The generator of this <principal ideal> has norm
$$
N_{K/\mathbb Q}(3+\sqrt2)=9-2=7.
$$
Reduction modulo $P$ gives $\sqrt2\equiv-3\equiv4\pmod7$. The map below is surjective and has $P$ in its kernel; both the kernel and $P$ have index seven, the latter by the <ideal norm>, so they are equal. Hence it induces an isomorphism of <residue fields>
$$
\mathcal O_K/P\cong\mathbb F_7,
\qquad
a+b\sqrt2\longmapsto a+4b\pmod7.
$$
In particular,
$$
\varepsilon\equiv5\pmod P.
$$
In the <finite field> $\mathbb F_7$, the element $5$ has multiplicative order six, since
$$
5^2\equiv4,
\qquad
5^3\equiv-1,
\qquad
5^6\equiv1\pmod7.
$$

By part (b), every unit is $(-1)^\delta\varepsilon^n$ for $\delta\in\{0,1\}$ and $n\in\mathbb Z$. Since $-1\equiv5^3$, its residue is $5^{n+3\delta}$, which equals one exactly when
$$
n+3\delta\equiv0\pmod6.
$$
The resulting units are $\varepsilon^{6k}$ and $-\varepsilon^{6k+3}$, and these are precisely the even and odd powers of $-\varepsilon^3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G=\langle-\varepsilon^3\rangle
=\left\langle-(1+\sqrt2)^3\right\rangle
=\langle-7-5\sqrt2\rangle},
$$
which is the group of <units congruent to one modulo 3 plus square root two>.

= 21F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= a
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $p:\widehat X\to X$ be the covering map. The <uniqueness of lifts to a covering space> says that if $\widehat f_1,\widehat f_2:Y\to\widehat X$ satisfy
$$
p\circ\widehat f_1=f=p\circ\widehat f_2
$$
and $\widehat f_1(y_0)=\widehat f_2(y_0)$ at one point $y_0\in Y$, then
$$
\boxed{\widehat f_1=\widehat f_2}.
$$

For any $y\in Y$, choose a path $\gamma$ from $y_0$ to $y$, using path-connectedness of $Y$. Both $\widehat f_1\circ\gamma$ and $\widehat f_2\circ\gamma$ lift the same path $f\circ\gamma$ and have the same initial point. Uniqueness in the <path lifting theorem> makes the lifted paths equal, so their endpoints agree. Since $y$ was arbitrary, the two lifts agree everywhere.

= b
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $X=S^1\vee S^1$, the <wedge of two circles>, and label its oriented loops $a$ and $b$. For a fixed $n\geq3$, let
$$
\alpha=(1\ 2\ \cdots\ n),
\qquad
\beta=(1\ 2)
$$
be permutations of the fibre $\{1,\ldots,n\}$. Construct a labelled graph $\widehat X_n$ with these $n$ vertices, an oriented $a$-edge from $i$ to $\alpha(i)$, and an oriented $b$-edge from $i$ to $\beta(i)$. Map every vertex to the wedge point and each labelled edge homeomorphically onto its corresponding loop. Every vertex has exactly one incoming and one outgoing edge of each label, so this is an $n$-sheeted <permutation covering of a wedge of circles>
$$
p_n:\widehat X_n\longrightarrow X.
$$
It is connected because the $n$-cycle $\alpha$ acts transitively on the fibre.

A <deck transformation> induces a permutation $\gamma$ of the vertices that commutes with both monodromy permutations $\alpha$ and $\beta$. Conversely, such a permutation determines a deck transformation, by the <deck transformation group as a monodromy centralizer>. The permutations $\alpha$ and $\beta$ generate $S_n$: the conjugates $\alpha^j\beta\alpha^{-j}$ include the adjacent transpositions, which generate the <symmetric group>. Hence $\gamma$ centralizes $S_n$ and lies in its <center of a group>. For $n\geq3$, the center of $S_n$ is trivial, so $\gamma=1$. A deck transformation fixing one point is the identity by part (a), and therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Deck}(p_n)=\{1\}}.
$$

= c
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

No. The <monodromy action of a covering space> for a two-sheeted covering takes values in $S_2$. Its nonidentity transposition commutes with every subgroup of $S_2$, so the <deck transformation group as a monodromy centralizer> always contains that transposition. Equivalently, every double cover has a <deck involution of a double covering> that exchanges the two points in each fibre. Thus a two-sheeted cover cannot have trivial deck group.

= 22H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= a
{parent=22h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x=(x_n)\in\ell^p$ with $1\leq p<\infty$, let
$$
x^{(N)}=(x_1,\ldots,x_N,0,0,\ldots)\in F.
$$
Then
$$
\lVert x-x^{(N)}\rVert_p^p
=\sum_{n>N}|x_n|^p\longrightarrow0
$$
because the defining nonnegative series converges. Thus $F=c_{00}$ is dense in the <l-p sequence space> for every finite $p$; this is the <density of finitely supported sequences in l-p>.

It is not dense in the <l-infinity sequence space>. For the constant sequence $\mathbf1=(1,1,\ldots)$ and any finitely supported $y$, some tail of $y$ is zero, so
$$
\lVert\mathbf1-y\rVert_\infty=1.
$$
Hence $\mathbf1$ does not belong to the closure of $F$.

= b
{parent=22h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Given $x\in\ell^p$, choose a sequence $x^{(m)}\in F$ with $x^{(m)}\to x$, which is possible by part (a). The bound gives
$$
\lVert Tx^{(m)}-Tx^{(l)}\rVert_p
\leq C\lVert x^{(m)}-x^{(l)}\rVert_p,
$$
so $(Tx^{(m)})$ is a <Cauchy sequence>. Since $\ell^p$ is a <Banach space>, it converges. Define
$$
\widetilde Tx=\lim_{m\to\infty}Tx^{(m)}.
$$
The same estimate shows that this limit does not depend on the approximating sequence. Passing to the limit proves linearity and
$$
\lVert\widetilde Tx\rVert_p\leq C\lVert x\rVert_p,
$$
so $\lVert\widetilde T\rVert\leq C$. It agrees with $T$ on $F$. If two bounded extensions existed, their difference would vanish on the dense subspace $F$ and, by continuity, on its closure $\ell^p$. Thus the extension is unique. This is the <extension of a bounded linear operator from a dense subspace>.

= c
{parent=22h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $e_m$ denote the sequence with a one in coordinate $m$ and zeros elsewhere.

For $T_1$, one has $\lVert e_m\rVert_p=1$ but $\lVert T_1e_m\rVert_p=m$ for every $p$, including $p=\infty$. Thus $T_1$ is unbounded for every $p$.

For $T_2$, the sequence $T_2e_m$ has entries $-(m-1)$ and $m$ in coordinates $m-1$ and $m$, respectively. Hence $\lVert T_2e_m\rVert_p\geq m$, so $T_2$ is also unbounded for every $p$.

For $T_3$, coordinatewise division gives
$$
\lVert T_3x\rVert_p\leq\lVert x\rVert_p
$$
for every $1\leq p\leq\infty$. Thus $T_3$ is a bounded <diagonal operator on sequence space> for every $p$.

For $T_4$, write $a=(n^{-1/2})_{n\geq1}$. Then $T_4x=x_1a$, and coordinate evaluation satisfies $|x_1|\leq\lVert x\rVert_p$. By the <p-series> criterion, $a\in\ell^p$ exactly when $p>2$, while $a\in\ell^\infty$. Therefore $T_4$ is bounded precisely for
$$
2<p\leq\infty.
$$
For $p\leq2$, even $T_4e_1$ does not belong to $\ell^p$.

For $T_5$, let $q$ be the <Holder conjugate exponent> of $p$. <Holder inequality> gives, for finite $p$,
$$
\left|\sum_{j=1}^nx_j\right|
\leq n^{1/q}\lVert x\rVert_p,
$$
and consequently
$$
\lVert T_5x\rVert_p^p
\leq\lVert x\rVert_p^p
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n^{p/q}}{2^{np}}<\infty.
$$
For $p=\infty$,
$$
|(T_5x)_n|\leq\frac n{2^n}\lVert x\rVert_\infty
\leq\lVert x\rVert_\infty.
$$
Thus $T_5$ is bounded for every $1\leq p\leq\infty$. Altogether,
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
&T_1&T_2&T_3&T_4&T_5\\ \hline
\text{bounded on }\ell^p
&\text{never}&\text{never}&\text{all }p&2<p\leq\infty&\text{all }p
\end{array}}
$$

= d
{parent=22h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Assume for contradiction that $X$ is infinite-dimensional. Starting with $M_0=\{0\}$, apply <Riesz lemma> recursively to the proper closed finite-dimensional subspaces
$$
M_{n-1}=\operatorname{span}\{x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}\}
$$
to choose unit vectors $x_n$ satisfying
$$
\operatorname{dist}(x_n,M_{n-1})>\frac12.
$$
For $m<n$, the vector $x_m$ belongs to $M_{n-1}$, and therefore
$$
\lVert x_n-x_m\rVert>\frac12.
$$
Thus $(x_n)$ lies in the closed unit ball but has no <Cauchy subsequence>, hence no convergent subsequence. A compact metric space is <sequentially compact space>[sequentially compact], contradicting compactness of the unit ball. Therefore $X$ is finite-dimensional, proving that a <compact unit ball characterizes finite-dimensional normed spaces>.

= 23H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis of Functions}

= a
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For each $n\in\mathbb N$, the open interval
$$
\left(n-\frac12,n+\frac12\right)
$$
meets $\mathbb N$ only in $\{n\}$. Thus every singleton is open in the <subspace topology> on $\mathbb N$. Every subset $A\subseteq\mathbb N$ is the union $A=\bigcup_{n\in A}\{n\}$ and is therefore open. Hence the induced topology is the <discrete topology>:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T=\mathcal P(\mathbb N)}.
$$

= b
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Because every subset of $\mathbb N$ is open, the <Borel sigma-algebra of a discrete space> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal B(\mathbb N)=\mathcal P(\mathbb N)}.
$$
If $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb R$ or $f:\mathbb N\to[0,\infty]$ and $B$ is a Borel subset of the codomain, then $f^{-1}(B)$ is some subset of $\mathbb N$, hence is Borel. Thus every such function is a <Borel measurable function>.

= c
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Equip $\mathbb N$ with <counting measure> $\#$. For a nonnegative function $f:\mathbb N\to[0,\infty]$, define
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\geq1}f(n)
:=\int_{\mathbb N}f\,d\#
=\sup_{N\geq1}\sum_{n=1}^Nf(n)}.
$$
This lies in $[0,\infty]$ and agrees with the <series as a Lebesgue integral against counting measure>.

For $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$, define
$$
\sum_{n\geq1}f(n)=\int_{\mathbb N}f\,d\#
$$
when $f$ is integrable, which here means
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\geq1}|f(n)|<\infty}.
$$
Thus the complex sum is defined under <absolute convergence> and can be obtained by integrating the real and imaginary parts.

A <simple function> on $\mathbb N$ is a function with finite range, equivalently
$$
s=\sum_{j=1}^ma_j\mathbf1_{A_j}
$$
for a finite measurable partition by subsets $A_j\subseteq\mathbb N$. Its nonnegative sum is $\sum_ja_j\#A_j$. It is finite exactly when every level set with $a_j>0$ is finite, equivalently when $s$ has finite support after its zero level is discarded.

= d
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <Beppo Levi theorem>, or <monotone convergence theorem>, states that if $(f_n)$ is a sequence of nonnegative measurable functions on a <measure space> and
$$
f_n\uparrow f
$$
pointwise almost everywhere, then $f$ is measurable and
$$
\boxed{\int f\,d\mu
=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int f_n\,d\mu},
$$
where either side may be infinite.

Measurability of $f=\sup_nf_n$ follows from measurability of countable suprema. Since $f_n\leq f$, <monotonicity of the Lebesgue integral> gives
$$
\lim_n\int f_n\,d\mu\leq\int f\,d\mu.
$$
For the reverse inequality, let $s$ be a nonnegative <simple function> with $s\leq f$, and fix $0<c<1$. Define
$$
E_n=\{x:f_n(x)\geq c,s(x)\}.
$$
Then $E_n\uparrow X$ up to the null set on which convergence fails. Since $f_n\geq c,s\mathbf1_{E_n}$,
$$
\int f_n\,d\mu
\geq c\int_{E_n}s\,d\mu
\longrightarrow c\int s\,d\mu
$$
by <continuity from below of a measure>, applied to the finitely many level sets of $s$. Hence $\lim_n\int f_n\geq c\int s$. Letting $c\uparrow1$ and taking the supremum over all simple $s\leq f$, as in the definition of the <Lebesgue integral>, gives the reverse inequality and proves the theorem.

= e
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For integer $k\geq1$, define $f_k(n)=f(k,n)$. The assumed monotonicity in $t$ gives
$$
f_k(n)\uparrow\lim_{t\to\infty}f(t,n)
$$
for every $n$. Apply the <monotone convergence theorem> on $\mathbb N$ with counting measure:
$$
\lim_{k\to\infty}\sum_{n\geq1}f(k,n)
=\sum_{n\geq1}\lim_{k\to\infty}f(k,n)
=\sum_{n\geq1}\lim_{t\to\infty}f(t,n).
$$
The function $t\mapsto\sum_nf(t,n)$ is itself nondecreasing, and the positive integers are cofinal in $\mathbb R$ as $t\to\infty$, so its limit over real $t$ equals its limit over integer $k$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{
\lim_{t\to\infty}\sum_{n\geq1}f(t,n)
=\sum_{n\geq1}\lim_{t\to\infty}f(t,n)}.
$$

Without monotonicity, take
$$
f(t,n)=\mathbf1_{[n,n+1)}(t)
\qquad(t\geq1).
$$
For each fixed $n$, $f(t,n)\to0$, so the sum of the pointwise limits is zero. At every $t\geq1$, however, exactly one term is one, so $\sum_nf(t,n)=1$ and its limit is one. This <moving-spike obstruction to interchanging a limit and an infinite sum> gives the required counterexample.

= 24F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= a
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let
$$
F(x,y)=x^3y+y^3+x.
$$
Its complex partial derivatives are
$$
F_x=3x^2y+1,
\qquad
F_y=x^3+3y^2.
$$
There is no point of $X'$ at which both vanish. Indeed, if $F=F_x=F_y=0$, then $F_y=0$ and $F=0$ give
$$
x^3=-3y^2,
\qquad
x=2y^3.
$$
Neither $x$ nor $y$ can be zero because $F_x=0$. Substitution into $F_x=0$ gives $12y^7+1=0$, whereas substitution into $F_y=0$ gives $8y^7+3=0$; these equations are incompatible.

For every $p=(x_0,y_0)\in X'$, at least one partial derivative is nonzero. If $F_y(p)\ne0$, the <holomorphic implicit function theorem> expresses the curve near $p$ as $y=g(x)$, and the restriction of the $x$-projection is a holomorphic coordinate chart. If $F_x(p)\ne0$, use the $y$-projection instead. On overlaps, the transition functions are restrictions of holomorphic coordinate functions and are holomorphic. These charts define an atlas making $X'$ a one-dimensional <complex manifold>, hence a <Riemann surface>.

= b
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Homogenizing the affine equation gives the <projective closure>
$$
C=\{[X:Y:Z]\in\mathbb P^2:
X^3Y+Y^3Z+Z^3X=0\}.
$$
This is the <Klein quartic>. It is smooth: if all three projective partial derivatives vanished, then
$$
3X^2Y=-Z^3,
\qquad
3Y^2Z=-X^3,
\qquad
3Z^2X=-Y^3.
$$
Multiplying them gives $28X^3Y^3Z^3=0$. If one coordinate is zero, the displayed equations force all three to vanish, which is impossible in projective space. Thus $C$ is a smooth compactification of $X'$. The smooth compactification compatible with the extended coordinate functions is its normalization, and since $C$ is already smooth it is biholomorphic to $C$.

For a direct genus computation, consider the extended coordinate map $\pi_x:X\to\mathbb C_\infty$. A generic $x$ leaves the cubic equation
$$
y^3+x^3y+x=0,
$$
so $\deg\pi_x=3$. In the affine part, ramification occurs where $F_y=0$. Solving $F=F_y=0$ gives $(0,0)$ and the seven points
$$
y^7=-\frac38,
\qquad
x=2y^3.
$$
At the origin, $x=-y^3+O(y^6)$, so the <ramification index of a holomorphic map> is three and the contribution is two. At each of the other seven points, $F_x=-7/2\ne0$, $F_y=0$, and $F_{yy}=6y\ne0$, so the ramification index is two and each contributes one.

There are two points at infinity. Near $P=[1:0:0]$, in coordinates $u=Y/X$, $v=Z/X$, the equation is
$$
u+u^3v+v^3=0,
$$
and $1/x=v$, so $P$ is unramified. Near $Q=[0:1:0]$, in coordinates $r=X/Y$, $s=Z/Y$, the equation is
$$
r^3+s+rs^3=0.
$$
Thus $s=-r^3+O(r^{10})$ and $1/x=s/r=-r^2+O(r^9)$, so $Q$ has ramification index two and contributes one. The total ramification is therefore
$$
2+7+1=10.
$$
The <Riemann-Hurwitz formula> for the degree-three map to the sphere gives
$$
2g(X)-2=3(2\cdot0-2)+10=4,
$$
and hence
$$
\boxed{g(X)=3}.
$$
This also agrees with the <genus of a smooth plane curve> of degree four.

= c
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/7}$ and define
$$
\phi(x,y)=(\zeta^3x,\zeta y).
$$
Each term in the defining polynomial is multiplied by $\zeta^3$:
$$
(\zeta^3x)^3(\zeta y)+(\zeta y)^3+\zeta^3x
=\zeta^3(x^3y+y^3+x).
$$
Thus $\phi$ is a holomorphic automorphism of $X'$, with inverse obtained by replacing $\zeta$ by $\zeta^{-1}$. By the assumption it extends to a holomorphic automorphism of $X$; in the projective model it is
$$
[X:Y:Z]\longmapsto[\zeta^3X:\zeta Y:Z].
$$
It has order exactly seven because $\zeta$ is primitive and $y$ is not identically zero. Hence $\operatorname{Aut}(X)$ contains an element of order seven.

The extended coordinate function $\pi_x:X\to\mathbb C_\infty$ is a <meromorphic function as a holomorphic map to the Riemann sphere>. Compose it with the seventh-power map of the sphere and set
$$
\pi=\pi_x^7.
$$
This map is nonconstant and holomorphic as a map to $\mathbb C_\infty$. Since $\pi_x\circ\phi=\zeta^3\pi_x$,
$$
\boxed{\pi\circ\phi
=(\zeta^3\pi_x)^7
=\pi_x^7
=\pi}.
$$

