The irreducible <affine hypersurface> $y^2-z^3=0$ in the $(y,z)$-plane has dimension one, while a closed point has dimension zero. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\dim C=1,
\qquad
\dim\{P\}=\dim\{Q\}=0}.
$$

= b
{parent=25f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose a $K$-basis $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ of $L$ and write
$$
\alpha=\sum_{i=1}^nx_ie_i,
$$
thereby identifying $L$ with $\mathbb A_K^n$. Let $M(x)$ be the $n\times n$ matrix whose $j$th column is the coordinate vector of $\alpha^{j-1}$ in this basis, for $1\leq j\leq n$.

Write the multiplication table as
$$
e_ie_j=\sum_{l=1}^nc_{ij}^{,l}e_l
$$
with fixed <structure constants> $c_{ij}^{,l}\in K$. Repeated multiplication shows that every entry of $M(x)$ is a polynomial in $x_1,\ldots,x_n$. Hence
$$
D(x)=\det M(x)\in K[x_1,\ldots,x_n].
$$
By the criterion supplied in the question,
$$
K[\alpha]=L
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
1,\alpha,\ldots,\alpha^{n-1}\text{ are a basis}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
D(x)\ne0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{U=D(D)=\mathbb A_K^n\setminus V(D)},
$$
which is a <distinguished open set>, and therefore a <Zariski-open set>. This is the determinant construction showing that <primitive elements form a principal Zariski-open set>. No assertion that $D$ is nonzero is needed.

= 26H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= a
{parent=26h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Define the <smooth function>
$$
F:\mathbb R^{n+1}\longrightarrow\mathbb R,
\qquad
F(x)=\lVert x\rVert^2.
$$
At $x\in F^{-1}(1)$ its derivative is
$$
D_xF(v)=2x\mathbin\cdot v.
$$
This linear map is surjective because $x\ne0$; for example, $D_xF(x)=2$. Hence one is a <regular value>, and the <regular level set theorem> makes
$$
S^n=F^{-1}(1)
$$
an embedded codimension-one submanifold of $\mathbb R^{n+1}$. Its tangent space is the kernel of the derivative:
$$
\boxed{T_xS^n
=\ker D_xF
=\{v:x\mathbin\cdot v=0\}
=x^\perp}.
$$
This is the <unit sphere as a regular level set>.

= b
{parent=26h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\operatorname{Sym}_n$ be the vector space of real symmetric matrices and consider
$$
\Phi:M_n(\mathbb R)\longrightarrow\operatorname{Sym}_n,
\qquad
\Phi(A)=A^TA.
$$
At $R\in O(n)$,
$$
D_R\Phi(H)=R^TH+H^TR.
$$
This derivative is surjective: for $S\in\operatorname{Sym}_n$, choosing $H=RS/2$ gives $D_R\Phi(H)=S$. Thus $I$ is a regular value and the <orthogonal group> $O(n)=\Phi^{-1}(I)$ is a submanifold of codimension
$$
\dim\operatorname{Sym}_n=\frac{n(n+1)}2.
$$
The determinant takes only the values $\pm1$ on $O(n)$, so the <special orthogonal group> $SO(n)=\{R\in O(n):\det R=1\}$ is an open-and-closed submanifold of $O(n)$. Therefore
$$
\dim SO(n)=n^2-\frac{n(n+1)}2=\frac{n(n-1)}2.
$$

The tangent space is the kernel of $D_R\Phi$:
$$
\boxed{T_RSO(n)
=\{H:R^TH+H^TR=0\}
=\{RA:A\in\mathfrak{so}(n)\}},
$$
where the <special orthogonal Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{so}(n)$ is the vector space of <skew-symmetric matrices>. This proves the <special orthogonal group as a submanifold> description.

= c
{parent=26h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose $Q\in SO(n+1)$ with $Qe_{n+1}=v$. Conjugation by $Q$ identifies
$$
S_v=\{R\in SO(n+1):Rv=v\}
$$
with
$$
\left\{
\begin{pmatrix}A&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}:A\in SO(n)
\right\}.
$$
It follows that the <unit-vector stabilizer in a special orthogonal group> is an embedded submanifold diffeomorphic to $SO(n)$, and
$$
\boxed{\dim S_v=\frac{n(n-1)}2}.
$$
At $R\in S_v$, its tangent space is
$$
T_RS_v=\{RA:A^T=-A,\ Av=0\}.
$$

For any $v\ne w$, both $S_v$ and $S_w$ contain the identity. At the identity,
$$
T_IS_v=\{A\in\mathfrak{so}(n+1):Av=0\},
\qquad
T_IS_w=\{A\in\mathfrak{so}(n+1):Aw=0\}.
$$
Identify $\mathfrak{so}(n+1)$ with the exterior square $\Lambda^2\mathbb R^{n+1}$. The orthogonal complement of $T_IS_v=\Lambda^2(v^\perp)$ is $v\wedge v^\perp$. If $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent, the nonzero bivector $v\wedge w$ belongs to the orthogonal complements of both tangent spaces. Their sum is therefore a proper subspace of $T_ISO(n+1)$, so the intersection is not <transverse intersection>[transverse]. If $w=-v$, then $S_w=S_v$, whose tangent space is proper, so they are again not transverse. Consequently
$$
\boxed{S_v\text{ and }S_w\text{ are never transverse when }v\ne w},
$$
as summarized by <distinct unit-vector stabilizers are not transverse>.

= 27K
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $d=1$ and $X\sim N(0,\sigma^2)$, the <characteristic function> is
$$
\boxed{\varphi_X(u)=e^{-\sigma^2u^2/2}}.
$$
For a <multivariate normal distribution> with covariance matrix $\sigma^2I_d$, the coordinates are uncorrelated and hence independent by <independence of uncorrelated jointly normal variables>. Therefore
$$
\varphi_X(u)
=\prod_{j=1}^d\mathbb E[e^{iu_jX_j}]
=\prod_{j=1}^de^{-\sigma^2u_j^2/2}
=\boxed{\exp\left(-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\sum_{j=1}^du_j^2\right)}.
$$
This is the <characteristic function of an isotropic Gaussian vector>.

= b
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an <orthogonal matrix> $A$, invariance in distribution gives
$$
\varphi_X(A^Tu)
=\varphi_{AX}(u)
=\varphi_X(u).
$$
The orthogonal group is transitive on each Euclidean sphere, so $\varphi_X(u)$ depends only on $\lVert u\rVert^2$. Define
$$
f(r)=\varphi_X(\sqrt r,e_1),
\qquad r\geq0.
$$
The <continuity of a characteristic function> makes $f$ continuous. Taking $A=-I$ also gives $X\stackrel d=-X$, and hence
$$
\varphi_X(u)=\varphi_X(-u)=\overline{\varphi_X(u)},
$$
so $f$ is real-valued. Thus
$$
\boxed{\varphi_X(u)=f(u_1^2+\cdots+u_d^2)},
$$
as for every <orthogonally invariant random vector>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Independence of the coordinates gives factorization of their joint <characteristic function of a random vector>. Orthogonal invariance under coordinate permutations makes all coordinate marginals identical. Hence, for $r_1,r_2\geq0$ and $d\geq2$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
f(r_1+r_2)
&=\varphi_X(\sqrt{r_1}e_1+\sqrt{r_2}e_2)\\
&=\varphi_{X_1}(\sqrt{r_1})
  \varphi_{X_2}(\sqrt{r_2})\\
&=f(r_1)f(r_2).
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{f(r_1+r_2)=f(r_1)f(r_2)}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

First, $f(0)=1$. For every $r\geq0$,
$$
f(r)=f(r/2)^2\geq0.
$$
It cannot vanish: if $f(r_0)=0$ for some $r_0>0$, then
$$
0=f(r_0)=f(r_0/n)^n
$$
would imply $f(r_0/n)=0$ for every positive integer $n$, contradicting continuity at zero. Thus $f(r)>0$. Since every characteristic function has modulus at most one,
$$
\boxed{0<f(r)\leq1}.
$$

Now set $h(r)=-\log f(r)$. It is continuous, nonnegative, and additive:
$$
h(r_1+r_2)=h(r_1)+h(r_2).
$$
The classification of a <continuous additive function on the nonnegative real numbers> gives $h(r)=\alpha r$, where $\alpha=h(1)\geq0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{f(r)=e^{-\alpha r}\qquad(r\geq0)}.
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Combining the preceding parts,
$$
\varphi_X(u)=e^{-\alpha\lVert u\rVert^2}.
$$
This is the characteristic function of $N(0,2\alpha I_d)$. The <uniqueness theorem for characteristic functions> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{X\sim N(0,2\alpha I_d)}.
$$
When $\alpha=0$, this means the degenerate distribution concentrated at the origin. This proves the <Maxwell characterization of the normal distribution>.

= 28K
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= a
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Conditional on $Y_m=i$, the <holding time> $V_m=U_m/g(i)$ has an <exponential distribution> of rate $g(i)$, and after that holding time the next state is $j$ with <transition probability> $p_{ij}$. The <memoryless property> of exponential random variables and the <Markov property> of $(Y_m)$ therefore make $(X_t)$ a <continuous-time Markov chain>.

Moreover $g(i)<1/\varepsilon$, so $V_m\geq\varepsilon U_m$. The <strong law of large numbers> gives $\sum_mU_m=\infty$ almost surely, hence $J_m\to\infty$ and the process is <nonexplosion under uniformly bounded jump rates>[nonexplosive]. Its <Q-matrix> is
$$
\boxed{q_{ij}=g(i)p_{ij}\quad(i\ne j),\qquad q_{ii}=-g(i).}
$$
Equivalently, if $D_g$ is the <diagonal matrix> with entries $g(i)$, then
$$
\boxed{Q=D_g(P-I).}
$$

= b
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <continuous-time Markov chain> is <irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible] if every state can reach every other state with positive probability. A state $i$ is <recurrent state>[recurrent] if, after leaving $i$, the chain returns to $i$ <almost surely>[with probability one]; an irreducible chain is recurrent if one, and hence every, state is recurrent. A recurrent state is <positive recurrent state>[positive recurrent] if its expected continuous <first return time> is finite, and an irreducible chain is positive recurrent when all its states are.

= c
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <jump chain> of $(X_t)$ is exactly $(Y_m)$: from $i$ it visits $j$ next with probability $p_{ij}$. Consequently the two chains have exactly the same directed paths of positive-probability transitions. Thus
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is irreducible}\iff Y\text{ is irreducible}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Because $J_m\to\infty$ almost surely, no infinite portion of the <jump chain> is hidden inside a finite time interval. After either chain leaves a state $i$, the event that it ever returns is therefore the same event for both chains. Hence <irreducibility and recurrence pass between a CTMC and its jump chain> gives
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is recurrent}\iff Y\text{ is recurrent}.}
$$

= d
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $\nu$ be an <invariant distribution> for the <continuous-time Markov chain>. Using $Q=D_g(P-I)$, its <invariant distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain>[stationarity equation] $\nu Q=0$ becomes
$$
(\nu D_g)P=\nu D_g.
$$
Thus the measure with weights $\nu_i g(i)$ is invariant for the irreducible positive-recurrent <jump chain>. Its <invariant distribution> $\pi$ is unique, so $\nu_i g(i)=c\pi_i$. Normalization gives the <invariant-measure transfer between a jump chain and a CTMC> formula
$$
\boxed{\nu_i=\frac{\pi_i/g(i)}{\displaystyle\sum_{j\in S}\pi_j/g(j)}}.
$$
The denominator is finite and nonzero because $\varepsilon<g(j)<1/\varepsilon$. Thus $\nu$ is a <probability distribution> and $(X_t)$ is positive recurrent.

= 29J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= a
{parent=29j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Gaussian likelihood> is proportional to
$$
\exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\lVert Y-X\theta\rVert_2^2\right),
$$
so <maximum likelihood estimation> is equivalent to <ordinary least squares>. If $p\leq n$ and $X$ has <rank> $p$, then $X^TX$ is invertible and the <normal equations> give
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{\rm MLE}=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY.}
$$
If $p>n$, then $\operatorname{rank}X<p$ and $\ker X\ne\{0\}$. A least-squares minimizer always exists, but it is not unique: by <rank-deficient ordinary least squares>, the complete set is
$$
X^+Y+\ker X,
$$
an <affine subspace> of dimension $p-\operatorname{rank}X>0$. Thus there are uncountably infinitely many likelihood maximizers. In the generic full-row-rank case their dimension is $p-n$.

= b
{parent=29j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiating the <ridge regression> objective gives
$$
\nabla_\theta\bigl(\lVert Y-X\theta\rVert_2^2+\lambda\lVert\theta\rVert_2^2\bigr)
=2(X^TX+\lambda I_p)\theta-2X^TY.
$$
For every nonzero $z$,
$$
z^T(X^TX+\lambda I_p)z=\lVert Xz\rVert_2^2+\lambda\lVert z\rVert_2^2>0,
$$
so $X^TX+\lambda I_p$ is <positive-definite matrix>[positive definite] and hence invertible. The strictly convex objective therefore has the unique <closed-form ridge regression estimator>
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_\lambda=(X^TX+\lambda I_p)^{-1}X^TY.}
$$

= c
{parent=29j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Because every column of $X$ has <sample mean> zero, every column $u_i$ of $U=XV$ also has sample mean zero. The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> gives
$$
U^TU=V^TX^TXV=V^T\Sigma V=\Lambda.
$$
Hence for $i\ne j$ the <sample covariance> is $n^{-1}u_i^Tu_j=0$, while the <sample variance> of $u_i$ is
$$
\frac1n u_i^Tu_i=\frac{\Lambda_{ii}}n.
$$
Thus the $u_i$ are pairwise uncorrelated <sample principal components>, ordered by decreasing sample variance.

= d
{parent=29j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Since $X=UV^T$ and $X^TX=V\Lambda V^T$,
$$
\widehat Y_{\rm MLE}
=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY
=U\Lambda^{-1}U^TY.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widehat Y_{\rm MLE}=U\Lambda^{-1}U^TY.}
$$
Put $w_i=u_i/\sqrt{\Lambda_{ii}}$. By part (c), the $w_i$ are <normalized sample principal components> forming an <orthonormal set>, and
$$
U\Lambda^{-1}U^T=\sum_{i=1}^p w_iw_i^T.
$$
This is the <orthogonal projection onto a finite-dimensional subspace> spanned by the $w_i$, so $\widehat Y_{\rm MLE}$ is the unique closest point in that subspace to $Y$.

= e
{parent=29j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Using the estimator from part (b) and the <spectral decomposition> of $X^TX$,
$$
\widehat Y_\lambda
=XV(\Lambda+\lambda I_p)^{-1}V^TX^TY
=U(\Lambda+\lambda I_p)^{-1}U^TY.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat Y_\lambda=U(\Lambda+\lambda I_p)^{-1}U^TY
=\sum_{i=1}^p\frac{\Lambda_{ii}}{\Lambda_{ii}+\lambda}w_iw_i^TY.}
$$
Under the stated separation of scales, the shrinkage factor is approximately one for $i\leq q$ and approximately zero for $i>q$. The <principal-component shrinkage by ridge regression> formula therefore reduces approximately to
$$
\widehat Y_\lambda\approx\sum_{i=1}^q w_iw_i^TY,
$$
the <orthogonal projection> of $Y$ onto the span of the $q$ normalized sample principal components with greatest variance.

= 30K
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=30k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The pair $(M_n,\mathcal F_n)_{n\geq0}$ is a <martingale> if $(\mathcal F_n)$ is a <filtration>, $(M_n)$ is an <adapted process>, every $M_n$ is <integrable random variable>[integrable], and
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[M_n\mid\mathcal F_{n-1}]=M_{n-1}\quad\text{almost surely for every }n\geq1.}
$$

= b
{parent=30k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The process is adapted to its <natural filtration>. At each fixed time it has finite support and is therefore integrable. Conditional on $X_{n-1}=0$,
$$
\mathbb E[X_n\mid X_{n-1}=0]
=\frac1{2n}-\frac1{2n}=0=X_{n-1}.
$$
Conditional on $X_{n-1}\ne0$,
$$
\mathbb E[X_n\mid X_{n-1}]
=\frac1n(nX_{n-1})+\left(1-\frac1n\right)0
=X_{n-1}.
$$
The <Markov property> now gives
$$
\mathbb E[X_n\mid\mathcal F_{n-1}]
=\mathbb E[X_n\mid X_{n-1}]=X_{n-1},
$$
so $(X_n)$ is a <martingale>.

= c
{parent=30k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume (i), and let $\tau$ be a <stopping time>. The increments of the <stopped martingale in discrete time>[stopped process] satisfy
$$
M_{(n+1)\wedge\tau}-M_{n\wedge\tau}
=\mathbf1_{\{\tau>n\}}(M_{n+1}-M_n).
$$
Because $\{\tau>n\}\in\mathcal F_n$, taking <conditional expectation> given $\mathcal F_n$ makes the right-hand side zero. Integrability follows from
$$
M_{n\wedge\tau}=M_0+\sum_{k=1}^n\mathbf1_{\{\tau\geq k\}}(M_k-M_{k-1}),
$$
a finite sum of integrable random variables. Hence $M^\tau$ is a martingale, proving (ii).

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume (ii). A <martingale> has constant <expected value> by the <tower property of conditional expectation>, so for every <stopping time> $\tau$ and every $n$,
$$
\mathbb E[M_{n\wedge\tau}]=\mathbb E[M_0].
$$
This is (iii).

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Assume (iii). Fix $n\geq0$ and $A\in\mathcal F_n$, and define
$$
T=n\mathbf1_A+(n+1)\mathbf1_{A^c}.
$$
This is a <stopping time>: $\{T\leq n\}=A\in\mathcal F_n$. Applying (iii) at time $n+1$ first to $T$ and then to the deterministic stopping time $n+1$ gives
$$
\mathbb E[\mathbf1_AM_n+\mathbf1_{A^c}M_{n+1}]
=\mathbb E[M_0]
=\mathbb E[M_{n+1}].
$$
Subtracting yields
$$
\mathbb E[\mathbf1_A(M_{n+1}-M_n)]=0
\qquad(A\in\mathcal F_n).
$$
By the defining characterization of <conditional expectation>, this says $\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]=M_n$. Thus $M$ is a martingale, proving (i). Together the three implications establish the <characterization of a martingale by stopped expectations>.

= 31E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=31E}

Let $\phi_t(x)$ denote the <flow map>[flow] through $x$. Its <alpha-limit set> and <omega-limit set> are
$$
\alpha(x)=\bigcap_{T<0}\overline{\{\phi_t(x):t<T\}},
\qquad
\omega(x)=\bigcap_{T>0}\overline{\{\phi_t(x):t>T\}}.
$$
Equivalently, they contain the limits of $\phi_{t_j}(x)$ along sequences $t_j\to-\infty$ and $t_j\to+\infty$, respectively.

= i
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <vector field> is unchanged by reflection in the $x$-axis, $(x,y)\mapsto(x,-y)$; more generally, linearity of the second equation gives the scaling symmetry $(x,y)\mapsto(x,cy)$. It is also reversible under reflection in the $y$-axis:
$$
(x(t),y(t))\longmapsto(-x(-t),y(-t)).
$$
When $k=0$, $y$ is constant, so arbitrary translations $y\mapsto y+c$ are additional symmetries.

= ii
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At an <equilibrium point>, $x^2-1=0$ and $kxy=0$. If $k\ne0$, the only equilibria are
$$
(1,0),\qquad(-1,0).
$$
Their <jacobian matrix> is
$$
DF(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}2x&0\\ky&kx\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $(1,0)$ its <eigenvalues> are $2,k$, and at $(-1,0)$ they are $-2,-k$. Hence for $k>0$, $(1,0)$ is an <unstable node> and $(-1,0)$ is a <stable node>; for $k<0$, both points are <saddle equilibrium>[saddles].

If $k=0$, every point on each line $x=1$ and $x=-1$ is an equilibrium. The line $x=1$ is transversely repelling because the nonzero eigenvalue is $2$, while $x=-1$ is transversely attracting because the nonzero eigenvalue is $-2$; the tangential eigenvalue is zero on both nonisolated equilibrium lines.

= iii
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Away from $x=\pm1$, the sign of $\dot x=x^2-1$ is fixed within each of the three invariant strips, so $x(t)$ is strictly monotone. On $x=\pm1$, the equation is $\dot y=\pm ky$, whose nonconstant solutions are exponential and hence not periodic. Therefore the <monotone coordinate excludes periodic orbits>, and there are
$$
\boxed{\text{no nonconstant periodic orbits for any }k.}
$$

= iv
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For $x\ne\pm1$, eliminating time gives the <separable differential equation>
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{kxy}{x^2-1},
$$
and hence
$$
y=C|x^2-1|^{k/2}.
$$
Define the possible limit at $x=s\in\{-1,1\}$ for a trajectory starting off the boundary lines by
$$
L_s=
\begin{cases}
\{(s,0)\},&k>0\text{ or }y_0=0,\\
\{(s,y_0)\},&k=0,\\
\varnothing,&k<0\text{ and }y_0\ne0.
\end{cases}
$$
The $k<0$ set is empty because $|y|\to\infty$, so there is no finite accumulation point. Since $\dot x<0$ on $(-1,1)$ and $\dot x>0$ outside $[-1,1]$, the complete classification off the boundary lines is
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{initial }x_0&\alpha(x_0,y_0)&\omega(x_0,y_0)\\ \hline
x_0<-1&\varnothing&L_{-1}\\
-1<x_0<1&L_1&L_{-1}\\
x_0>1&L_1&\varnothing.
\end{array}
$$
For $x_0>1$ the solution has forward <finite-time blow-up of an ordinary differential equation>[finite-time blow-up], and for $x_0<-1$ it has backward finite-time blow-up, explaining the other empty entries under the usual infinite-time definitions.

It remains to treat $x_0=s=\pm1$. Here $x(t)=s$ and $y(t)=y_0e^{kst}$. If $y_0=0$, both limit sets equal $\{(s,0)\}$. If $y_0\ne0$, then
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
ks&\alpha(s,y_0)&\omega(s,y_0)\\ \hline
ks>0&\{(s,0)\}&\varnothing\\
ks=0&\{(s,y_0)\}&\{(s,y_0)\}\\
ks<0&\varnothing&\{(s,0)\}.
\end{array}
$$
This includes all boundary cases in both $k$ and the initial point.

= v
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The initial point has $x_0=0$, so its orbit remains in $-1<x<1$. Integrating the orbit equation from part (iv) and using $y(0)=y_0$ gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=y_0(1-x^2)^{k/2},\qquad -1<x<1.}
$$
This formula also covers $y_0=0$ and $k=0$ within the open strip. The endpoints are approached only as infinite time limits and have the behavior classified in part (iv).

= 32C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= a
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In canonical coordinates, <Hamilton's equations> are
$$
\boxed{\dot q_i=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i},\qquad
\dot p_i=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i}\quad(1\leq i\leq n).}
$$
A function $F(q,p)$ is an <integral of motion> if it is constant along every <Hamiltonian flow>, equivalently
$$
\dot F=\{F,H\}=0
$$
for a time-independent $F$. The system is an <integrable Hamiltonian system> when it has $n$ first integrals $F_1,\ldots,F_n$ whose <total differential>[differentials] are linearly independent almost everywhere and which are in mutual <Poisson bracket>[Poisson involution]: $\{F_i,F_j\}=0$ for all $i,j$.

= b
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For one degree of freedom, take $F_1=H$. Antisymmetry of the <Poisson bracket> gives $\{H,H\}=0$, so <Hamiltonian conservation law>[energy is conserved]. The stated condition is exactly $dH\ne0$, which supplies the required functional independence. Hence the system is integrable at every such regular point.

= c
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $F_1,\ldots,F_n$ be commuting independent integrals for $(M,H)$ and let $G_1,\ldots,G_m$ be such integrals for $(\widetilde M,\widetilde H)$. Regard each as a function on the product <phase space>. Then
$$
\{F_i,H+\widetilde H\}=\{F_i,H\}=0,
\qquad
\{G_a,H+\widetilde H\}=\{G_a,\widetilde H\}=0.
$$
The $F_i$ commute among themselves, the $G_a$ commute among themselves, and every cross bracket $\{F_i,G_a\}$ vanishes because the functions depend on disjoint canonical coordinates. Their differentials form two independent coordinate blocks, so all $n+m$ are independent on the product of the two regular sets. Thus the combined system is integrable, an instance of the <direct product of integrable Hamiltonian systems>.

= d
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write the <Hamiltonian> as the sum of separated one-dimensional energies
$$
H=\sum_{i=1}^n H_i,
\qquad
H_i=\frac12\left(p_i^2+\omega_i^2q_i^2\right).
$$
Each $(q_i,p_i)$ system is a <simple harmonic oscillator> and is integrable away from $(q_i,p_i)=(0,0)$ by part (b). The separated energies satisfy
$$
\{H_i,H_j\}=0,
$$
and their differentials are independent whenever every oscillator pair is nonzero. The <Poisson-commuting separated energies> therefore make the $n$-dimensional oscillator integrable. The exceptional coordinate strata have measure zero, so this proves <Liouville integrability> in its usual almost-everywhere sense; the origin is the only point where $dH$ itself vanishes.

= e
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

No. Consider two uncoupled oscillators with nonzero amplitudes:
$$
q_i(t)=A_i\cos(\omega_it+\phi_i),
\qquad
p_i(t)=-A_i\omega_i\sin(\omega_it+\phi_i),
\qquad i=1,2.
$$
The orbit is bounded. If it had a <periodic orbit>[period] $T>0$, then $\omega_1T$ and $\omega_2T$ would both be integer multiples of $2\pi$, forcing $\omega_1/\omega_2\in\mathbb Q$. Choosing an irrational frequency ratio gives an <incommensurate harmonic-oscillator orbit>, which is quasiperiodic on an <invariant torus> and not periodic.

= 33B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33B}

The <canonical commutation relations> are $[X_i,X_j]=[P_i,P_j]=0$ and $[X_i,P_j]=i\hbar\delta_{ij}$. Substitution into the definitions of the <creation and annihilation operators> gives
$$
\boxed{[A_i^\dagger,A_j^\dagger]=0,\qquad[A_i,A_j]=0,\qquad[A_i,A_j^\dagger]=\delta_{ij}.}
$$

The normalized <ground state> is defined by
$$
A_i|0\rangle=0\quad(i=1,2,3),
\qquad \langle0|0\rangle=1.
$$
Repeated use of $[A_i,A_i^\dagger]=1$ gives the <Cartesian number state of the three-dimensional isotropic harmonic oscillator>
$$
\boxed{|n_1,n_2,n_3\rangle
=\frac{(A_1^\dagger)^{n_1}(A_2^\dagger)^{n_2}(A_3^\dagger)^{n_3}}
{\sqrt{n_1!n_2!n_3!}}|0\rangle,}
$$
with <energy eigenvalue>
$$
E_{n_1n_2n_3}=\hbar\omega\left(n_1+n_2+n_3+\frac32\right).
$$

Inverting the ladder-operator definitions yields
$$
X_i=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2\mu\omega}}(A_i+A_i^\dagger),
\qquad
P_i=i\sqrt{\frac{\mu\hbar\omega}{2}}(A_i^\dagger-A_i).
$$
The antisymmetry of the <Levi-Civita symbol> then cancels the two-creation and two-annihilation terms in $\mathbf L=\mathbf X\times\mathbf P$, leaving the <orbital angular momentum in oscillator ladder operators>
$$
\boxed{L_i=-i\hbar\varepsilon_{ijk}A_j^\dagger A_k.}
$$
Write $|j\rangle=A_j^\dagger|0\rangle$. Then
$$
L_i|j\rangle=-i\hbar\varepsilon_{ikj}|k\rangle,
\qquad
L^2|j\rangle=2\hbar^2|j\rangle.
$$
Since the <orbital angular momentum> eigenvalue is $\ell(\ell+1)\hbar^2$, every first-excited state has
$$
\boxed{\ell=1.}
$$
Finally,
$$
L_z|x\rangle=i\hbar|y\rangle,
\qquad
L_z|y\rangle=-i\hbar|x\rangle.
$$
Therefore the normalized <magnetic quantum number> $m=+1$ state can be chosen as
$$
\boxed{|\psi\rangle=\frac{|x\rangle+i|y\rangle}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{A_x^\dagger+iA_y^\dagger}{\sqrt2}|0\rangle,}
$$
which obeys $L_z|\psi\rangle=\hbar|\psi\rangle$ and $\lVert|\psi\rangle\rVert=1$.

= 34B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=34b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

= b
{parent=34b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

= 35D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= a
{parent=35d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Macroscopically, an <adiabatic process> exchanges no <heat> with its surroundings: $\delta Q=0$. A reversible adiabatic process is therefore <isentropic process>[isentropic], since $dS=\delta Q_{\rm rev}/T=0$. Microscopically, the system is thermally isolated, so changes in its energy arise from work done by changing external parameters rather than random energy exchange with a <heat bath>; in a sufficiently slow change, the populations of the corresponding microscopic states are carried along without producing entropy.

For repeated operation the working substance must execute a <thermodynamic cycle>. Reversible isothermal contact at $T_2$ absorbs heat $Q_2$, a reversible adiabat changes the temperature to $T_1$, reversible isothermal contact at $T_1$ rejects heat $Q_1>0$, and a second adiabat returns to the initial state. This is the <Carnot cycle>. Reversibility and zero net entropy change give
$$
\frac{Q_2}{T_2}-\frac{Q_1}{T_1}=0,
\qquad
\frac{Q_1}{Q_2}=\frac{T_1}{T_2}.
$$
The <thermal efficiency> is the net work output divided by the absorbed heat:
$$
\eta=\frac{W}{Q_2}=\frac{Q_2-Q_1}{Q_2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\eta_{\max}=1-\frac{T_1}{T_2}.}
$$
The <Carnot theorem> shows that any irreversible cycle has smaller efficiency, so the two isotherms joined by two adiabats are optimal.

= b
{parent=35d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At fixed volume, <entropy from heat capacity> obeys $dS=C(T)dT/T$. The <third law of thermodynamics> fixes $S(0)=0$ when the ground state is nondegenerate, so below the transition
$$
S(T_c^-)=\int_0^{T_c}\frac{\alpha T}{T}\,dT=\alpha T_c.
$$
Above the transition,
$$
S(T)=S(T_c^+)+\int_{T_c}^{T}\frac\beta{T'}\,dT'
=S(T_c^+)+\beta\log\frac{T}{T_c}.
$$
For a transition with no <latent heat>, entropy is continuous, and comparison with $S=\beta\log T+\gamma$ gives the directly testable relation
$$
\boxed{\gamma=\alpha T_c-\beta\log T_c.}
$$
Thus calorimetric measurements of $C(T)$ and $T_c$ verify $\gamma$. More generally, if a measurable latent heat $L$ is present, $S(T_c^+)-S(T_c^-)=L/T_c$, and the formula becomes
$$
\boxed{\gamma=\alpha T_c+\frac{L}{T_c}-\beta\log T_c.}
$$

= 36E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= Solution
{parent=36E}

For a constant <electromagnetic field tensor>, the <relativistic Lorentz force> is a linear system with constant coefficients, so
$$
\boxed{u^\mu(\tau)=
\left[\exp\!\left(\frac qmF\tau\right)\right]^\mu{}_{\nu}u^\nu(0).}
$$

Put
$$
a=\frac{qE}{mc},
\qquad
b=\frac{qB}{m},
\qquad
\gamma_0=\frac1{\sqrt{1-v_0^2/c^2}}.
$$
The initial <four-velocity> is $(u^0,u^1,u^2,u^3)=\gamma_0(c,0,v_0,0)$. For parallel fields along $x$, the field tensor has one hyperbolic block in the $(0,1)$ plane and one rotation block in the $(2,3)$ plane. With the orientation convention in the question,
$$
u^0=\gamma_0c\cosh(a\tau),
\qquad
u^1=\gamma_0c\sinh(a\tau),
$$
$$
u^2=\gamma_0v_0\cos(b\tau),
\qquad
u^3=-\gamma_0v_0\sin(b\tau).
$$
Taking the initial spacetime point to be the origin and integrating $u^\mu=dx^\mu/d\tau$ gives the <relativistic motion in parallel electric and magnetic fields>
$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
t(\tau)&=\frac{\gamma_0}{a}\sinh(a\tau),\\
x(\tau)&=\frac{\gamma_0c}{a}\bigl(\cosh(a\tau)-1\bigr),\\
y(\tau)&=\frac{\gamma_0v_0}{b}\sin(b\tau),\\
z(\tau)&=\frac{\gamma_0v_0}{b}\bigl(\cos(b\tau)-1\bigr).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The expressions have their continuous $a\to0$ or $b\to0$ limits when one field vanishes; reversing the sign of $q$ reverses the electric acceleration and magnetic rotation.

Eliminating <proper time> between the first two equations yields
$$
\boxed{x(t)=\frac{\gamma_0c}{a}
\left[\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{at}{\gamma_0}\right)^2}-1\right].}
$$
At early coordinate time,
$$
x(t)=\frac{qE}{2m\gamma_0}t^2+O(t^4),
$$
which is uniformly accelerated motion with the transverse relativistic inertia factor $\gamma_0$. At late positive time,
$$
x(t)=\operatorname{sgn}(qE)ct-\frac{\gamma_0mc^2}{qE}+O(t^{-1}),
$$
so the longitudinal speed approaches the <speed of light>.

The $y$-$z$ projection satisfies
$$
y^2+\left(z+\frac{\gamma_0v_0}{b}\right)^2
=\left(\frac{\gamma_0v_0}{b}\right)^2.
$$
It is therefore a circle of radius and proper-time period
$$
\boxed{R=\frac{\gamma_0m|v_0|}{|q|B},
\qquad
\Delta\tau=\frac{2\pi m}{|q|B}.}
$$

For the pitch magnitude assume $v_0>0$ and put $\alpha=|q|E/(mc)$, $\Omega=|q|B/m$, and
$$
r=\alpha\Delta\tau=\frac{2\pi E}{cB}.
$$
Number the $n$th proper-time period as $[(n-1)\Delta\tau,n\Delta\tau]$. Its longitudinal displacement is
$$
\Delta x_n=\frac{\gamma_0c}{\alpha}
\bigl[\cosh(nr)-\cosh((n-1)r)\bigr].
$$
Consequently, as $n\to\infty$,
$$
P_n=\frac{\Delta x_n}{R}
\sim\frac{c\Omega}{2\alpha v_0}(1-e^{-r})e^{nr}
=A\exp\!\left(\frac{2\pi En}{cB}\right),
$$
where
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{c^2B}{2Ev_0}
\left(1-e^{-2\pi E/(cB)}\right).}
$$

